<usx version="3.1">
  <book code="JOB" style="id">EN_UST en_English_ltr Tue Oct 17 2023 09:03:19 GMT-0400 (Eastern Daylight Time) tc</book>
  <para style="ide">UTF-8
</para>
  <para style="h">Job
</para>
  <para style="toc1">The Book of Job
</para>
  <para style="toc2">Job
</para>
  <para style="toc3">Job
</para>
  <para style="mt1">Job

</para>
  <ms style="ts"/>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="JOB 1"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 1:1"/>In the land of Uz there lived a man whose name was Job. He was a man of complete integrity. He respected God and so he avoided doing evil things.
<verse eid="JOB 1:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 1:2"/>Now Job had seven sons and three daughters.
<verse eid="JOB 1:2"/><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 1:3"/>He owned 7, 000 sheep, 3, 000 camels, 1, 000 oxen, and 500 donkeys. He also had many servants. Job was richer than anyone else who lived in the area east of the Jordan River.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:3"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 1:4"/>Job’s sons often held feasts in their homes. Whenever any of them held a feast, he would invite all of his brothers and sisters to share the meal with him.
<verse eid="JOB 1:4"/><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 1:5"/>After all of the sons had each held a feast, Job would gather his children together. He would ask Yahweh to cleanse them from any action they might have committed during their feasting that would have made them unacceptable to him. He would get up early in the morning, kill animals, and burn them on the altar {as sacrifices}, one for each {of his children}. {For} Job {always said}, “Perhaps my children have sinned by saying something evil about God in their thoughts. {If they have, I need to ask God to forgive them.}” Job did this after each time his sons had all hosted feasts.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:5"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 1:6"/>One day the angels came and gathered together in front of Yahweh, and Satan came too.
<verse eid="JOB 1:6"/><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 1:7"/>Yahweh asked Satan, “Where have you just come from?”

</para>
  <para style="p">Satan replied, “I have come from the earth, where I was traveling around {to see what was happening}.”

<verse eid="JOB 1:7"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 1:8"/>Yahweh said to Satan, “Have you noticed Job, who worships me? I mention him because no one else on earth has such complete integrity as he does. He respects me and so he refuses to do anything evil.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:8"/><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 1:9"/>Satan replied to Yahweh, “Job only worships you because of what you have done for him.
<verse eid="JOB 1:9"/><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 1:10"/>You have always protected him, his family, and everything he owns. You make him succeed in whatever work he does. As a result, he has built very large herds of livestock.
<verse eid="JOB 1:10"/><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 1:11"/>But if you attack what he owns and take it away, he will certainly curse you in front of everyone.”

<verse eid="JOB 1:11"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 1:12"/>Yahweh replied to Satan, “All right, I permit you to take away everything that he has. But do not harm him in his own body.”

</para>
  <para style="p">Satan {agreed and then} left Yahweh {in order to carry out his attack on Job}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:12"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 1:13"/>One day after that, Job’s sons and daughters were attending a feast at the home of the oldest brother.
<verse eid="JOB 1:13"/><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 1:14"/>{While they were doing that,} a messenger arrived at Job’s home. He told Job, “Your oxen were plowing the fields and your donkeys were grazing nearby.
<verse eid="JOB 1:14"/><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 1:15"/>But a group of men, Sabeans, came and attacked us. They killed all of your servants who were working in the fields. They took away all the oxen and donkeys! I am the only one who has escaped to come and tell you what happened.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:15"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 1:16"/>While he was still talking to Job, another messenger arrived. He said to Job, “Lightning from the sky struck and killed all the sheep and all the men who were taking care of them! I am the only one who has escaped to come and tell you what happened.”

<verse eid="JOB 1:16"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 1:17"/>While he was still talking to Job, a third messenger arrived. He said to Job, “Three groups of robbers from the region of Chaldea came and attacked us. They stole all the camels and killed all the men who were taking care of them. I am the only one who has escaped to come and tell you what happened.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:17"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 1:18"/>While he was still talking to Job, a fourth messenger arrived. He said to Job: “Your sons and daughters were feasting in the home of their oldest brother.
<verse eid="JOB 1:18"/><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 1:19"/>Suddenly a very strong wind came from the desert and struck the house. The house collapsed on your sons and daughters and killed them all! I am the only one who has escaped to come and tell you what happened.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 1:19"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 1:20"/>Then Job stood up and tore his robe and shaved his head {because he was very sad}. Then he lay down on the ground to worship God.
<verse eid="JOB 1:20"/><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 1:21"/>He said,
</para>
  <para style="q1">“When I was born, I was wearing no clothes.
</para>
  <para style="q1">When I die, I will not take any clothes with me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It is Yahweh who gave me everything that I possessed,
</para>
  <para style="q1">and it is Yahweh who has taken it all away.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I find no fault with Yahweh.”

<verse eid="JOB 1:21"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 1:22"/>So in spite of all the things that had happened to him, Job did not sin by saying that what God had done was wrong.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 1:22"/><verse eid="JOB 1:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 1"/>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" sid="JOB 2"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 2:1"/>On another day, the angels came again and gathered together in front of Yahweh. Satan also came again.
<verse eid="JOB 2:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 2:2"/>Yahweh asked Satan, “Where have you come from {this time}?” Satan replied, “I have come from the earth, where I was traveling around {to see what was happening}.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 2:2"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 2:3"/>Yahweh asked Satan, “Have you noticed that Job continues to worship me? I mention him again because no one else on earth has such complete integrity as he does. He respects me, and so he refuses to do anything evil. He still does this even though you persuaded me to {permit you to} attack him for no reason.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 2:3"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 2:4"/>Satan replied to Yahweh, “Job still worships you because he is glad you destroyed his herds and family rather than destroying him. People will give up everything they have in order to save their own lives.
<verse eid="JOB 2:4"/><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 2:5"/>But if you harm his body, he will certainly curse you in front of everyone!”

<verse eid="JOB 2:5"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 2:6"/>Yahweh replied to Satan, “All right, I permit you to harm his body. But you must leave him alive.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 2:6"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 2:7"/>Satan {agreed and he} left. He went and caused Job to suffer from very painful boils all over his body.
<verse eid="JOB 2:7"/><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 2:8"/>Job took a piece of broken pottery and scraped the boils on his skin. He sat down in a pile of ashes {outside the city}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 2:8"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 2:9"/>His wife said to him, “You should not still be loyal to God! You should curse God and then go ahead and die.”

<verse eid="JOB 2:9"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 2:10"/>But Job replied, “You are talking the way people talk who do not know God. We should be willing to receive whatever God sends us.” So in spite of all the things that had happened to him, Job did not sin by saying that what God had done was wrong.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 2:10"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 2:11"/>Job had some good and wise friends. One of them was Eliphaz from the town of Teman. Another was Bildad, who was a Shuhite. A third friend was Zophar from the town of Naamah. These men heard about the terrible things that had happened to Job. So they each left their homes and went together to visit Job to comfort him sincerely.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 2:11"/><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 2:12"/>But when they saw Job from a distance, they almost did not recognize him. They wailed loudly, they all tore their clothing, and they threw dust into the air that settled on their heads. {They did these things to demonstrate how sorry they were for Job.}
<verse eid="JOB 2:12"/><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 2:13"/>Then they sat on the ground with Job for seven days. {During that time} none of them said anything to him, out of respect for how greatly he was suffering.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 2:13"/><verse eid="JOB 2:13"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 2"/>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" sid="JOB 3"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 3:1"/>At the end of those seven days, Job started speaking. He cursed the day when he was born.
<verse eid="JOB 3:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 3:2"/>He said,
<verse eid="JOB 3:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 3:3"/>“I wish that the day of my birth had never existed!
</para>
  <para style="q2">{I feel the same way about} the night when my mother conceived me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 3:4"/>I wish that there would only be darkness on that day {so that it would not even be a day}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that God in heaven would not care about that day
</para>
  <para style="q2">and so not make the sun shine on it.
<verse eid="JOB 3:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 3:5"/>I wish that that day were only darkness,
</para>
  <para style="q2">with clouds blocking out the sunlight
</para>
  <para style="q2">and many other things also making it very dark.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 3:6"/>I wish that the moon and stars would not shine on the night when my mother conceived me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that the day of my birth was not part of any month.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Indeed, I wish that it was not a day in the year at all.
<verse eid="JOB 3:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 3:7"/>I mean it—I wish that no mother would ever again bear a child on that date.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that no one would ever again rejoice {over a child’s birth} on that date.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 3:8"/>There are magicians who know how to agitate the chaos monster. I want them to curse the day of my birth!

<verse eid="JOB 3:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 3:9"/>I wish that the stars that shone early in the morning of that day would never shine again.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that it would never become light on that day.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that not even the first rays of the rising sun would appear on that day.
<verse eid="JOB 3:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 3:10"/>I wish that day did not exist, because my mother gave birth to me on that day,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and because I became alive, I have now experienced all these terrible things.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 3:11"/>I wish that I had died right after my mother gave birth to me!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish I had stopped breathing after being alive for only a short time!
<verse eid="JOB 3:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 3:12"/>I wish that my mother had never held me on her lap and nursed me!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 3:13"/>If I had died shortly after my mother gave birth to me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would now be resting peacefully among all the other people who had died.
<verse eid="JOB 3:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 3:14"/>{I would be resting} with great kings and their advisers.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They built beautiful tombs on spacious grounds {and are now resting in them}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 3:15"/>Or {I would be resting} with princes
</para>
  <para style="q2">who owned great quantities of gold and silver.
<verse eid="JOB 3:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 3:16"/>I wish that I had died in my mother’s womb
</para>
  <para style="q2">and never lived for even a moment.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then people would have buried me privately.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 3:17"/>After wicked people die, they do not cause any more trouble.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That allows the people whom they were oppressing to rest.
<verse eid="JOB 3:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 3:18"/>Those who were prisoners all rest peacefully {after they die}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They no longer have slave drivers ordering them around.
<verse eid="JOB 3:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 3:19"/>Everyone, whether rich or poor, goes to the realm of the dead.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those who were slaves no longer have to obey their masters.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 3:20"/>God should not allow those who are suffering greatly like me to continue to remain alive!
</para>
  <para style="q2">God should not allow those who are very miserable to live!
<verse eid="JOB 3:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 3:21"/>They long to die, but they do not die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They desire to die more than people desire to find buried treasure.
<verse eid="JOB 3:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 3:22"/>{God should not give life in the first place} to people who are very happy when they finally die!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 3:23"/>God should not give life to people if he is going to keep them from knowing what to do
</para>
  <para style="q2">or from understanding what is really happening to them!
<verse eid="JOB 3:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 3:24"/>I am wishing that I were dead because now I cry so much that I cannot eat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I just keep groaning loudly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 3:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 3:25"/>There were things that I always worried might happen to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those things have now happened!
<verse eid="JOB 3:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 3:26"/>I really have no peace of mind at all.
</para>
  <para style="q2">My troubles keep disturbing me.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 3:26"/><verse eid="JOB 3:26"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 3"/>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" sid="JOB 4"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 4:1"/>Then Eliphaz, Job’s friend from the town of Teman, replied to what Job had said. Eliphaz said,
<verse eid="JOB 4:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 4:2"/>“Will you please allow me to say something to you?
</para>
  <para style="q2">I feel compelled to speak to you.
<verse eid="JOB 4:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 4:3"/>You yourself have given good advice to many people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You have encouraged people who were discouraged.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 4:4"/>When people were struggling, you were able to say the right thing to help them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You enabled them to be brave again.
<verse eid="JOB 4:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 4:5"/>But now that you yourself are suffering, you have become discouraged.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You are not facing your troubles bravely {as you have helped others to do}.
<verse eid="JOB 4:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 4:6"/>You should be confident because you honor God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You should be hopeful because you conduct yourself with integrity.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 4:7"/>Think about this: God does not destroy people who are innocent!
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, God never kills people who are doing what is right!
<verse eid="JOB 4:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 4:8"/>I know from experience that this is true:
</para>
  <para style="q2">When people plan to do bad things to others,
</para>
  <para style="q2">those bad things happen to them instead.
<verse eid="JOB 4:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 4:9"/>God becomes very angry with them {because of the wrong things they plan to do},
</para>
  <para style="q2">and so God commands that they will die, and they do die.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 4:10"/>Wicked people may boast and threaten,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but God destroys them.
<verse eid="JOB 4:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 4:11"/>Those wicked people will have nothing in the end, like lions that starve to death because God keeps them from catching any prey.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Different relatives will have to take care of the children of those wicked people, as if they were lion cubs whose parents caught no prey and they had to separate from each other in order to search for food.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 4:12"/>I once heard a message that someone came and whispered to me.
<verse eid="JOB 4:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 4:13"/>One night I fell deeply asleep and had a dream that made me very perplexed.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 4:14"/>I was so scared that I started to shake all over.
<verse eid="JOB 4:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 4:15"/>Then a spirit slid into view right in front of me!
</para>
  <para style="q2">This caused the hair on the back of my neck to stand straight up.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 4:16"/>The spirit stopped there, but I could not see what it really looked like.
</para>
  <para style="q2">However, I knew that there was some being in front of me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">At first it did not speak, but then it said,
<verse eid="JOB 4:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 4:17"/>‘No human being can be more righteous than God.
</para>
  <para style="q1">No person can be better than his Creator.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 4:18"/>Consider this: God recognizes that even his own angels may not always do what is right.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If they do not act wisely, he corrects them.
<verse eid="JOB 4:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 4:19"/>So God is even more careful to discipline human beings,
</para>
  <para style="q2">knowing how very frail they are.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 4:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 4:20"/>{Humans are so fragile that} they can be here today and gone tomorrow.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{And humans are so insignificant that} a person can die, never to return to earth, and no one will even notice.
<verse eid="JOB 4:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 4:21"/>Humans are {fragile,} like tents that collapse if someone pulls up their stakes.
</para>
  <para style="q2">And they may live their whole lives on earth without ever learning to be wise.’

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 4:21"/><verse eid="JOB 4:21"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 4"/>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" sid="JOB 5"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 5:1"/>If you ask someone to judge between you and God, no one will agree to do that for you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Not even an angel would have the authority or wisdom to judge your case.
<verse eid="JOB 5:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 5:2"/>People who do not respect God get angry with God {instead of considering how they have sinned}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Naïve people resent God {when they do not get what they think they deserve}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{Their anger and resentment lead them to disobey God, so that} God punishes them severely and even kills them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So respect God and do not be naïve!
<verse eid="JOB 5:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 5:3"/>I have seen foolish people who seemed to be successful.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But immediately I recognized that they would not prosper for long.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 5:4"/>Their children are never safe.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They always lose to their opponents in court
</para>
  <para style="q2">because no one defends them.
<verse eid="JOB 5:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 5:5"/>People who do not respect God are not able to harvest the crops that they plant.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Poor people come and claim the grain—right to the edges of the field!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, poor people see their crops and are glad to have them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 5:6"/>After all, trouble does not come out of nowhere.
<verse eid="JOB 5:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 5:7"/>No, people cause trouble for themselves throughout their lives.
</para>
  <para style="q2">This is as certain as the fact that sparks shoot up from a fire.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 5:8"/>If I were suffering as you are,
</para>
  <para style="q1">I would ask God for help
</para>
  <para style="q2">and trust God to help me.
<verse eid="JOB 5:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 5:9"/>God does great things that we can not understand.
</para>
  <para style="q2">We can not even count the marvelous things that God does.
<verse eid="JOB 5:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 5:10"/>God is the one who sends rain onto the earth
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that it waters our fields.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 5:11"/>God defends people who are humble.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He gives widows safe places to live.
<verse eid="JOB 5:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 5:12"/>God keeps devious people from being able
</para>
  <para style="q2">to do what they plan.
<verse eid="JOB 5:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 5:13"/>When people try cleverly to make others suffer,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God makes this cause suffering for them instead.
</para>
  <para style="q1">When people try to trick others,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God shows that it is a trick before it can fool the others.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 5:14"/>God keeps wicked people from knowing how to fulfill their plans.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It is as if the wicked people are always in the dark, even during the day,
</para>
  <para style="q1">and they need to feel around with their hands to try to find where to go.
<verse eid="JOB 5:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 5:15"/>When powerful people threaten or accuse weak people,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God rescues the weak people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He keeps the powerful people from harming them.
<verse eid="JOB 5:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 5:16"/>{Because they know that God will help them,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">poor people can confidently expect that good things will happen to them.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God proves that they are innocent
</para>
  <para style="q2">when people accuse them falsely.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 5:17"/>Consider this: Anyone whom God corrects is better off because of it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So you should be thankful that God is disciplining you.
<verse eid="JOB 5:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 5:18"/>You should be thankful because
</para>
  <para style="q2">God allows people to suffer {in order to correct them}
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then he restores them.

<verse eid="JOB 5:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 5:19"/>Whenever you are in trouble, God will keep rescuing you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will keep bad things from happening to you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 5:20"/>When there is little food to eat, God will not allow you to starve to death.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When there is war, God will not allow your enemies to kill you.
<verse eid="JOB 5:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 5:21"/>God will protect you when people say bad things about you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will protect you from things that could badly harm you.
<verse eid="JOB 5:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 5:22"/>You will not worry about things like that
</para>
  <para style="q2">or about not having enough to eat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will not be afraid that wild animals will hurt you,

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 5:23"/>because you will be confident that those wild animals will not attack you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will not have big rocks in your fields that make plowing difficult.
<verse eid="JOB 5:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 5:24"/>You will know that things will go well for you in your home.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When you check on your animals, you will see that they are all there.
<verse eid="JOB 5:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 5:25"/>You will be certain that you will have many descendants.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will be as numerous as blades of grass.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 5:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 5:26"/>You will live a long life,
</para>
  <para style="q2">like grain that grows to become fully ripe before it is harvested.
<verse eid="JOB 5:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 5:27"/>I can assure you that my friends and I have thought carefully about these things.
</para>
  <para style="q1">We know that they are true,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so please pay careful attention to what I have said!
</para>
  <para style="q1">You also will realize that it is true.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 5:27"/><verse eid="JOB 5:27"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 5"/>
  <chapter number="6" style="c" sid="JOB 6"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 6:1"/>Then Job replied to Eliphaz and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 6:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 6:2"/>“If I could put all my troubles and misery
</para>
  <para style="q2">on a scale and weigh them,
<verse eid="JOB 6:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 6:3"/>they would certainly be heavier than all the sand along the ocean shores.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That is why I spoke very rashly about the day when I was born.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 6:4"/>It is as if Almighty God had shot me with arrows.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if those arrows had poison on their tips and that poison had gone into my spirit.
</para>
  <para style="q1">The things that God has done to me are like soldiers lined up to attack me.
<verse eid="JOB 6:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 6:5"/>Just as a wild donkey does not complain by braying when it has plenty of grass to eat,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and an ox does not complain by bellowing when it has food to eat,
</para>
  <para style="q1">I would not complain if you were really helping me.
<verse eid="JOB 6:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 6:6"/>People use salt to help them eat food that has little taste.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People do that with the white of an egg, for example.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I have been saying how I feel in order to help me deal with my situation.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 6:7"/>But regarding food itself,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I actually do not want to eat at all,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because my troubles are making me too upset to eat.
<verse eid="JOB 6:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 6:8"/>I wish that God would do for me what I have asked of him:
<verse eid="JOB 6:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 6:9"/>I wish that God would agree to let me die!
</para>
  <para style="q2">God is certainly able to end my life.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 6:10"/>If he did that, I would be comforted:
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would know that in spite of the unrelenting pain I had suffered,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would still always have obeyed God’s commands.
<verse eid="JOB 6:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 6:11"/>I do not have the strength to endure until these troubles are over.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have nothing to look forward to, so I do not want to live any longer!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 6:12"/>I am not strong like stone,
</para>
  <para style="q2">my body is not sturdy like bronze,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so I will not be able to endure these troubles for a long time.
<verse eid="JOB 6:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 6:13"/>I am no longer able to help myself.
</para>
  <para style="q2">My troubles are keeping me from doing that.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 6:14"/>When a man has many troubles, his friends should be kind to him,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even if he stops honoring Almighty God.
<verse eid="JOB 6:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 6:15"/>But you, my friends, have not helped me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">You are like streams in the wilderness
</para>
  <para style="q2">that travelers cannot depend on,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because they leave their channels empty in the dry season.
<verse eid="JOB 6:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 6:16"/>These streams are full of water
</para>
  <para style="q2">when the melting ice and snow make them overflow,
<verse eid="JOB 6:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 6:17"/>but when the dry season comes, there is no water flowing in those streams,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and the channels dry up.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 6:18"/>Merchant caravans turn off the road to search for water.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{However, there is no water in those stream beds,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so} the merchants die in the desert.
<verse eid="JOB 6:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 6:19"/>The men in those caravans searched for some water
</para>
  <para style="q2">because they were sure that they would find some.
<verse eid="JOB 6:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 6:20"/>They were very disappointed,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because they had been sure that they would find water.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They traveled a long distance to the stream bed,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but there was no water in it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 6:21"/>Similarly, you three have not helped me at all!
</para>
  <para style="q1">You have seen that terrible things have happened to me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and you are not helping me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because you are afraid that God might do similar things to you.
<verse eid="JOB 6:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 6:22"/>{After I lost all my wealth,} I did not ask any of you to give me money.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I did not plead with any of you to spend some of your money to help me.
<verse eid="JOB 6:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 6:23"/>I never asked any of you to rescue me from my enemies.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, I did not ask you to save me from people who were oppressing me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 6:24"/>Answer me now, and then I will be quiet;
</para>
  <para style="q2">tell me what wrong things I have done!
<verse eid="JOB 6:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 6:25"/>I would be willing to listen to what you had to say, even if it were painful, as long as it was helpful.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But though you are trying to correct me, you are not really helping me at all.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 6:26"/>I am a man who has nothing to hope for,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but you try to correct me, and you think what I say is as useless as the wind!
<verse eid="JOB 6:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 6:27"/>In fact, you are treating me as callously as if you were gambling for an orphan
</para>
  <para style="q2">or arguing over the price of a friend you were selling into slavery!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 6:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 6:28"/>Please look at me! I will not lie while I am talking straight to you.
<verse eid="JOB 6:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 6:29"/>Please stop saying that I have sinned! Stop criticizing me unfairly!
</para>
  <para style="q2">You should realize that I have not done anything wrong.
<verse eid="JOB 6:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 6:30"/>You must not think that I am lying!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I know what is right to say and what is wrong to say.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 6:30"/><verse eid="JOB 6:30"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 6"/>
  <chapter number="7" style="c" sid="JOB 7"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 7:1"/>Life is very difficult for all of us on this earth!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Like manual laborers, our days are long and hard.
<verse eid="JOB 7:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 7:2"/>We are like slaves who keep wanting to be in the cool of evening.
</para>
  <para style="q2">We are like workers who have to wait to be paid.
<verse eid="JOB 7:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 7:3"/>Like a slave, I am enduring a time when it feels useless to be alive.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have been experiencing many miserable nights.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 7:4"/>When I go to bed I say, ‘I hope I will be able to sleep for a long time!’
</para>
  <para style="q2">But I never really get to sleep. I am restless right through to the morning.
<verse eid="JOB 7:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 7:5"/>My body is covered with maggots and dirt;
</para>
  <para style="q2">pus oozes out of my open sores.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 7:6"/>I am running out of days faster than a shuttle releases yarn into a loom,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I have no reason to hope that my life will end well.
<verse eid="JOB 7:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 7:7"/>God, remember that my life will soon end.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{I do not think that} I will ever be happy again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 7:8"/>God, you see me now,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but {after I die,} you will not see me anymore.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You may look for me then, but {you will not find me, because} I will not exist.
<verse eid="JOB 7:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 7:9"/>Just as a cloud never appears in the sky again once it fades,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so when people die and go to the place where dead people are, they never return to earth.
<verse eid="JOB 7:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 7:10"/>They never return and live in their houses again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 7:11"/>So I will say everything I want to say.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will speak because I am so upset.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will complain because I am so bitter.
<verse eid="JOB 7:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 7:12"/>God, you do not need to watch what I am doing so closely!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am not a dangerous sea monster!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 7:13"/>Sometimes I think that if I could just go to sleep, I would stop suffering;
</para>
  <para style="q2">my pain would be less while I was sleeping.
<verse eid="JOB 7:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 7:14"/>But then you make me have terrible nightmares!
<verse eid="JOB 7:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 7:15"/>And I wish someone would kill me by choking me
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that I would not have to stay alive {and suffer so much}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 7:16"/>I hate being alive {and suffering so much}!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not want to live any longer!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Stop afflicting me, because I will only be alive for a short time.
<verse eid="JOB 7:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 7:17"/>We human beings are not very important!
</para>
  <para style="q2">You should not think that we are so important that you need to pay attention to what we do.
<verse eid="JOB 7:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 7:18"/>You do not need to check every morning to see what we are doing!
</para>
  <para style="q2">You do not need to evaluate us at every moment!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 7:19"/>I wish you would stop watching me
</para>
  <para style="q2">and leave me alone for at least a little while!
<verse eid="JOB 7:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 7:20"/>You watch me constantly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but if I sin, that certainly does not harm you!
</para>
  <para style="q1">I feel as if I am your target and that you are shooting at me!
</para>
  <para style="q2">You act as if I am bothering you all the time!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 7:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 7:21"/>If I have done wrong things, you could just forgive me.

</para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <para style="q1">I am going to die soon.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When I do, then if you decide you want us to be friends, it will be too late, because I will be dead.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 7:21"/><verse eid="JOB 7:21"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 7"/>
  <chapter number="8" style="c" sid="JOB 8"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 8:1"/>Then Bildad, Job’s friend who was a Shuhite, responded to Job. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 8:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 8:2"/>“Job, you should not continue to talk like this.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You are saying a lot of things, but they are not sensible.
<verse eid="JOB 8:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 8:3"/>God always does things fairly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, Shaddai always does what is right.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 8:4"/>Your children sinned against God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">This is evident, because God has punished them for the evil things they did.
<verse eid="JOB 8:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 8:5"/>But as for you, you should pray to God. Yes, you should ask Shaddai to help you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 8:6"/>If you are genuinely good,
</para>
  <para style="q2">then God will certainly do something good for you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He will give you back the abundant prosperity that good people have.
<verse eid="JOB 8:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 8:7"/>God would make you so wealthy later in life
</para>
  <para style="q2">that it would seem as if you were not very prosperous before!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 8:8"/>You should really consider the wise teachings of the people who lived before us.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, you should think about what our ancestors learned about life.
<verse eid="JOB 8:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 8:9"/>I advise you to consider the wisdom of our ancestors
</para>
  <para style="q2">because we have only been alive for a short time
</para>
  <para style="q2">and we know very little.
</para>
  <para style="q1">This is because our time here on the earth is very short,
</para>
  <para style="q2">like a shadow that appears briefly and then goes away.
<verse eid="JOB 8:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 8:10"/>But if you consider the wisdom of our ancestors, you will learn useful things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Indeed, you will learn very profound things.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 8:11"/>Papyrus certainly does not grow away from marshland.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, reeds certainly cannot grow where there is no water.
<verse eid="JOB 8:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 8:12"/>If their water dries up while they are blossoming,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they wither, while plants that do have water keep flourishing.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 8:13"/>People who do not pay attention to God are like those reeds.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Such people hope for good things, but they do not get them.
<verse eid="JOB 8:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 8:14"/>Godless people confidently expect good things to happen to them, but those things do not happen.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Godless people trust certain other people to help them, but those others prove unreliable.
<verse eid="JOB 8:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 8:15"/>Godless people think they are secure because they are wealthy,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but ultimately they can not rely on their wealth for security.
</para>
  <para style="q1">When they begin to fail despite their wealth, they try to preserve their status,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but they fail anyway.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 8:16"/>Godless people are like plants that formerly flourished in abundant sunshine,
</para>
  <para style="q2">whose branches once spread out over the ground.
<verse eid="JOB 8:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 8:17"/>Godless people are like plants whose roots once twisted securely around piles of stones
</para>
  <para style="q2">and clung tightly to rocks.
<verse eid="JOB 8:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 8:18"/>But a person can still pull a plant completely out of the ground even if it is that healthy and securely rooted.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then it will be as if the plant was never there in the first place.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People who do not pay attention to God will perish just as completely.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 8:19"/>So people who do not pay attention to God will be sad in the end.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Other people will come and take their places.
<verse eid="JOB 8:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 8:20"/>So I tell you, Job, that God will help you if you do what is right.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But God will not help you if you do what is wrong.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 8:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 8:21"/>God can still make you happy,
</para>
  <para style="q2">yes, very happy.
<verse eid="JOB 8:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 8:22"/>{When God blesses you,} everyone who opposed you will be very ashamed.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will completely destroy wicked people.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 8:22"/><verse eid="JOB 8:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 8"/>
  <chapter number="9" style="c" sid="JOB 9"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 9:1"/>Then Job replied to Bildad and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 9:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 9:2"/>“I certainly know that what you have said is true.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But a person can not prove that he has done the right thing
</para>
  <para style="q2">{and that God should not punish him}.
<verse eid="JOB 9:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 9:3"/>If someone wanted to argue with God about that,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God would ask him many questions
</para>
  <para style="q2">and that person would not be able to answer any of them!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 9:4"/>God thinks very deeply and he is very powerful.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one who has tried to argue against him has ever been able to win.
<verse eid="JOB 9:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 9:5"/>God moves mountains {in earthquakes} without anyone knowing in advance what he is going to do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God knocks down mountains when he is angry.
<verse eid="JOB 9:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 9:6"/>God causes earthquakes far below the surface of the earth
</para>
  <para style="q2">that shake the ground and make it move.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 9:7"/>On some days, God makes it so cloudy that people can not see the sun in the sky.
</para>
  <para style="q2">On some nights, God makes it so cloudy that people can not see the stars shining.
<verse eid="JOB 9:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 9:8"/>God created the sky all by himself.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God defeated the forces of chaos {so that he could make an orderly universe}.
<verse eid="JOB 9:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 9:9"/>God arranged the stars in the sky to make constellations such as the Bear, Orion, and the Pleiades.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God arranged stars into many other constellations as well.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 9:10"/>God does amazing things that people can not understand.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does more marvelous things than people are able to count.
<verse eid="JOB 9:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 9:11"/>If God passed by where I was, I would not be able to see him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, he might be going by, but I would not notice him.
<verse eid="JOB 9:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 9:12"/>If God wants to take something away, no one can stop him from doing that.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one can require God to defend what he is doing.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 9:13"/>God would continue to be angry {even if someone tried to calm him down}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{No one can oppose him;} he {even} defeated the forces of Rahab, the great sea monster.
<verse eid="JOB 9:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 9:14"/>So I could hardly think of the right words to say
</para>
  <para style="q2">in order to argue a case against God!
<verse eid="JOB 9:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 9:15"/>Even if I were innocent, I would not be able to argue a case against him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">All I could do would be to ask God, as my judge, to be merciful to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 9:16"/>Even if he agreed to hear my case,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would not believe that he was genuinely considering my arguments.
<verse eid="JOB 9:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 9:17"/>He keeps causing troubles that batter me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He hurts me many times for no good reason.
<verse eid="JOB 9:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 9:18"/>He causes me to suffer all the time.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if he will not even let me get my breath again {before he causes the next disaster}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 9:19"/>Suppose I did make a case against him. And suppose the winner would be the one who was stronger.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then he would win, because he is far stronger than I am.
</para>
  <para style="q1">And it would not matter who was right,
</para>
  <para style="q2">since no one could make him obey what a judge decided.
<verse eid="JOB 9:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 9:20"/>Even though I was innocent, he would find grounds in what I said to punish me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even though I had not done anything wrong, he would still decide, based on what I said, that I was guilty.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 9:21"/>I have not done anything wrong, {but that does not matter anymore because} I do not care what happens to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not care whether I live or die.
<verse eid="JOB 9:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 9:22"/>God punishes everyone the same way.
</para>
  <para style="q2">As I have been saying, God destroys everyone whether they have done wrong or not.
<verse eid="JOB 9:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 9:23"/>When people experience disaster and it causes them to die suddenly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God laughs when he sees them suffer, even if they are innocent.
<verse eid="JOB 9:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 9:24"/>God has allowed wicked people to control what happens to everyone else.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as though God has caused judges not to be able to judge fairly.
</para>
  <para style="q1">If it is not God who has done that,
</para>
  <para style="q2">who, then, has done it?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 9:25"/>I am approaching the end of my life more quickly than a messenger runs to bring news.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if the days of my life are running away from me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Good things do not happen to me.
<verse eid="JOB 9:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 9:26"/>My life is going by very rapidly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as fast as a sailing boat made from reeds,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as fast as an eagle swooping down to seize an animal.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 9:27"/>If I decide that I will just forget what I am complaining about,
</para>
  <para style="q2">if I decide to stop looking sad and try to be cheerful,
<verse eid="JOB 9:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 9:28"/>I still become afraid again
</para>
  <para style="q2">because I know that God does not consider me innocent
</para>
  <para style="q2">and so I think that he will punish me.
<verse eid="JOB 9:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 9:29"/>He will condemn me anyway,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so why should I keep trying in vain to defend myself?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 9:30"/>If I washed my body with pure water from melted snow
</para>
  <para style="q2">and cleansed my hands with strong soap
</para>
  <para style="q2">{to show how innocent I am},
<verse eid="JOB 9:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 9:31"/>God would just throw me into a filthy pit {to show how guilty he considers me to be}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">My body would become so dirty that it would be as if my own clothes did not want to be on it!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 9:32"/>God is not a human, as I am,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so I could not refute him to prove that I am innocent
</para>
  <para style="q2">if we went together to have a trial in a courtroom.
<verse eid="JOB 9:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 9:33"/>There is no one to mediate,
</para>
  <para style="q2">no one who has authority over both of us.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 9:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 9:34"/>There is no one who could keep God from causing me to suffer,
</para>
  <para style="q2">who could keep God from doing terrifying things that frighten me.
<verse eid="JOB 9:34"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="JOB 9:35"/>{If someone could protect me and judge between us,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would declare that I am innocent without being afraid of God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But I can not do that now.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 9:35"/><verse eid="JOB 9:35"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 9"/>
  <chapter number="10" style="c" sid="JOB 10"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 10:1"/>I am tired of living,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so I will freely express everything I want to complain about {without worrying about what God will do to me}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, I will say exactly how unhappy I am.
<verse eid="JOB 10:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 10:2"/>I will say to God, ‘If you are going to punish me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">you should tell me what I have done wrong!’
<verse eid="JOB 10:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 10:3"/>You seem to think that it is all right to harm me
</para>
  <para style="q2">and to abandon me, whom you created.
</para>
  <para style="q1">But at the same time, you seem pleased to allow
</para>
  <para style="q2">wicked people to do the things that they plan to do!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 10:4"/>Certainly you know and understand more than what people can see with their eyes! {So you should understand my true motives and know that I am innocent.}
<verse eid="JOB 10:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 10:5"/>You do not live for only a short time as people do!
<verse eid="JOB 10:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 10:6"/>So you do not need to act as if you would run out of time
</para>
  <para style="q2">if you did not discover all of my sins right away!
<verse eid="JOB 10:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 10:7"/>{You do not need to work hard to discover any sins that I may have committed,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because} you know that I am not guilty.
</para>
  <para style="q2">And there is no one who could help me get away from you {if you decided you should punish me}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 10:8"/>You personally created every single part of me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So it is not right for you to destroy someone whom you created yourself.
<verse eid="JOB 10:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 10:9"/>Please recall how you made me! You molded me the way people mold objects out of clay.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you destroy me, I will crumble into dust {and you will lose your marvelous creative work}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 10:10"/>You made me through a careful process,
</para>
  <para style="q2">the way people carefully make cheese out of milk.
<verse eid="JOB 10:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 10:11"/>You attached my bones together with tendons,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then you covered them with flesh inside my skin.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 10:12"/>You are the one who made me alive.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{It seemed that} you were faithfully taking care of me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{It seemed that} you were protecting me.
<verse eid="JOB 10:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 10:13"/>But you kept secret what you were planning to do to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am certain that you were planning to do these things to me!
<verse eid="JOB 10:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 10:14"/>You were watching me to see if I would sin
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that you could punish me for sinning.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 10:15"/>If I am a wicked man,
</para>
  <para style="q2">then I hope that terrible things will happen to me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">But even if I am a good man,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I must still act ashamed
</para>
  <para style="q2">because you are punishing me as if I had sinned.
<verse eid="JOB 10:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 10:16"/>If I did act like the good man that I am,
</para>
  <para style="q2">you would {conclude that you should punish me further
</para>
  <para style="q1">and you would} hunt for me the way a lion hunts for some animal to kill.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{When you caught me,} you would do even more things to harm me greatly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 10:17"/>You would claim that I had committed even more sins.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You would become even more angry with me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You would keep punishing me in new ways.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 10:18"/>God, you should not have allowed my mother to give birth to me!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that I had died at birth!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, I wish that my parents had never shown me to anyone.
<verse eid="JOB 10:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 10:19"/>I wish that I had never even existed!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that I had died at birth so that people had buried me right away.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 10:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 10:20"/>I am not going to live very much longer!
</para>
  <para style="q2">So please leave me alone and let me have a little peace.
<verse eid="JOB 10:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 10:21"/>{Let me have some peace now because}
</para>
  <para style="q2">soon I will go to the place from which I will never return.
</para>
  <para style="q1">In that place {where dead people go} it is always gloomy.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, it is very dark.
<verse eid="JOB 10:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 10:22"/>Yes, in that place it is very dark, the way it is in deep shadow.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Things are very chaotic there.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Even the small light that is there
</para>
  <para style="q2">is like darkness.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 10:22"/><verse eid="JOB 10:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 10"/>
  <chapter number="11" style="c" sid="JOB 11"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 11:1"/>Then Zophar, Job’s friend from the town of Naamah, responded to Job. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 11:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 11:2"/>“I would like to respond to the long speech that you have just given.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You talk a lot about how innocent you are, but that does not mean that you truly are innocent.
<verse eid="JOB 11:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 11:3"/>You have boasted that you are innocent,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I need to talk back in response.
</para>
  <para style="q1">You have said disrespectful things about God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I need to tell you that you should be ashamed!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 11:4"/>I need to correct you because you have told God,
</para>
  <para style="q2">‘I believe the right things,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and you know that I have done the right things.’
<verse eid="JOB 11:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 11:5"/>If only God would speak
</para>
  <para style="q2">and say something to correct you!
<verse eid="JOB 11:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 11:6"/>If only God would tell you the secrets that he knows because he is so wise!
</para>
  <para style="q2">{You need to realize that} God knows far more than you do.
</para>
  <para style="q1">If you could understand as God does,
</para>
  <para style="q2">you would realize that God is punishing you less than you deserve!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 11:7"/>You will never be able to understand God by thinking hard about him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, you will never be able to find out everything about Shaddai.
<verse eid="JOB 11:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 11:8"/>God’s wisdom is so much greater than ours,
</para>
  <para style="q2">it is as if it is higher than the sky.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So there is nothing you can do to understand it all.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It is as if God’s wisdom is deeper below the earth
</para>
  <para style="q2">than the place where dead people go.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So it is impossible for you to know it all.
<verse eid="JOB 11:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 11:9"/>There is so much to know about God
</para>
  <para style="q2">that it is as if that knowledge is longer than the earth
</para>
  <para style="q2">and wider than the ocean.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 11:10"/>If God wants to arrest you and put you on trial,
</para>
  <para style="q2">no one can stop him from doing that.
<verse eid="JOB 11:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 11:11"/>{God is a trustworthy judge} because he knows which people have bad character.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When God sees people doing wicked things, he punishes them.
<verse eid="JOB 11:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 11:12"/>Some people simply have no understanding.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will become wise when humans start having wild donkeys as babies{—that is, never!}

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 11:13"/>Job, I urge you to repent humbly
</para>
  <para style="q2">and pray sincerely to God.
<verse eid="JOB 11:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 11:14"/>If you have been doing evil things, stop doing them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you do nothing wrong in any part of your life,

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 11:15"/>then you will be able to act as a person who is not ashamed of anything.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will be strong, and you will not be afraid of anything.
<verse eid="JOB 11:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 11:16"/>You will not be afraid, because you will forget all the bad things that happened to you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will all be gone, like the water that flows away down a river.
<verse eid="JOB 11:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 11:17"/>Your life will be happy again.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It will be as if the sun were shining on you as brightly as it shines at mid-day.
</para>
  <para style="q1">You have had many troubles, but they will end,
</para>
  <para style="q2">just as night ends when the sun rises.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 11:18"/>You will feel safe because you will be confident that good things will happen to you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will think about your life and realize that you can go to bed and sleep peacefully.
<verse eid="JOB 11:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 11:19"/>You will lie down to sleep and nothing will scare you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Many people will come and ask you to be kind to them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 11:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 11:20"/>But God will punish wicked people by making them die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will not be able to escape from God’s punishment.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will hope to live, but they will die anyway.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 11:20"/><verse eid="JOB 11:20"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 11"/>
  <chapter number="12" style="c" sid="JOB 12"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 12:1"/>Then Job replied to Zophar and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 12:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 12:2"/>“You are speaking as if you were the people to whom everyone should listen.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You seem to believe that you are the only wise people who are now alive.
<verse eid="JOB 12:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 12:3"/>But I have as much good sense as you do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am just as wise as you are.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People certainly know, in general, the things that you have said.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 12:4"/>Before, when I prayed to God for help, God would help me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But now{, because God is not helping me in my suffering,} other people laugh at me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I am righteous and I honor God, but they laugh at me!
<verse eid="JOB 12:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 12:5"/>People like you who have no troubles think that others are suffering because of their sins.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You think that God is punishing us and that is why we are suffering.
<verse eid="JOB 12:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 12:6"/>Meanwhile, bandits get rich by robbing people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those who defiantly disobey God live peacefully.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Such people believe that God cannot stop them from doing what they want to do.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 12:7"/>But if the wild animals could speak,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and if you asked them {what they know about God},
</para>
  <para style="q2">they would tell you {that God is responsible when someone suffers as I am suffering}.
</para>
  <para style="q1">If the birds could speak,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and if you asked them {the same question},
</para>
  <para style="q2">they would tell you {the same thing}.
<verse eid="JOB 12:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 12:8"/>If the ground could talk, and if you asked it about God,
</para>
  <para style="q2">or if fish could talk and you asked them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they too would tell you that God is responsible when people suffer.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 12:9"/>All of them certainly know that it is Yahweh himself who has caused me to suffer.
<verse eid="JOB 12:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 12:10"/>He controls whether all living creatures live or die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">including whether any person dies or continues to live.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 12:11"/>We decide whether our food is good or bad by tasting it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">In the same way, we think carefully about what we hear people say in order to decide whether it is good or bad advice.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{I have listened carefully to you, and I have decided that you are giving me bad advice!}
<verse eid="JOB 12:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 12:12"/>Older people have usually become wise.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Because they have lived a long time, they have learned many things.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 12:13"/>God himself is wise and very powerful.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He has good sense and he understands everything.
<verse eid="JOB 12:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 12:14"/>Indeed, if God tears something down, no one can build it back up again.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If God puts someone in prison, no one can enable that person to escape.
<verse eid="JOB 12:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 12:15"/>Yes, God is so powerful that if he prevents rain from falling, then the water on the earth dries up.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If God causes a lot of rain to fall, then water floods onto the land.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 12:16"/>God is the one who is truly strong and wise.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He does not let anyone get away with doing wrong or with convincing others to do wrong.
<verse eid="JOB 12:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 12:17"/>{God is so powerful that} he can remove royal advisors from their positions,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he can cause judges to become fools.
<verse eid="JOB 12:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 12:18"/>God can take away the ruling authority that kings have
</para>
  <para style="q2">and make those kings become slaves instead.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 12:19"/>God can remove priests from their positions
</para>
  <para style="q2">and take the power away from those who have long ruled others.
<verse eid="JOB 12:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 12:20"/>God can cause trusted counselors not to know what to say,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he can cause wise elders no longer to have good sense.
<verse eid="JOB 12:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 12:21"/>God can cause princes to lose the respect of others,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he can cause those who are powerful to become weak.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 12:22"/>God can enable people to understand
</para>
  <para style="q2">things that are very difficult to understand.

<verse eid="JOB 12:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 12:23"/>God causes some nations to become very great,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but later he destroys them.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God enables some nations to enlarge their territory greatly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but later he allows others to conquer them and take their people away as prisoners.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 12:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 12:24"/>He causes some human rulers to become foolish.
</para>
  <para style="q2">As a result, they try one thing after another without success,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if they were lost in a desert without roads.
<verse eid="JOB 12:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 12:25"/>It is as if they were trying to feel their way in complete darkness.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if they were staggering around drunk.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 12:25"/><verse eid="JOB 12:25"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 12"/>
  <chapter number="13" style="c" sid="JOB 13"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 13:1"/>“I can assure you, my friends, that I already know everything that you have been telling me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, I have heard and understood all that you have said.
<verse eid="JOB 13:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 13:2"/>I know everything that you know.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not know less than you do.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 13:3"/>But I would like to speak with Almighty God{, not with you}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would like to convince him {that I am innocent}.
<verse eid="JOB 13:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 13:4"/>As for you, you are telling so many lies about me
</para>
  <para style="q2">that other people can hardly recognize what I am really like.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Your advice is so worthless
</para>
  <para style="q2">that you are all like doctors who sell people useless medicine.
<verse eid="JOB 13:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 13:5"/>I wish that you would just stop talking.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then you would really seem to be wise!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 13:6"/>Please pay attention to what I am telling you!
<verse eid="JOB 13:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 13:7"/>You are speaking on God’s behalf,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but you are not telling the truth!
</para>
  <para style="q1">You are speaking deceptively,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but you think that you are helping God!
<verse eid="JOB 13:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 13:8"/>You are not being fair to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Instead, you are acting as if you are God’s lawyers defending him in court!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 13:9"/>If God investigates what you are doing,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he will find that you are doing wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q1">No, you can not deceive him
</para>
  <para style="q2">as you might deceive a human being!
<verse eid="JOB 13:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 13:10"/>God would certainly rebuke you when he discovered that
</para>
  <para style="q2">you were pretending to be fair but were actually being unfair.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 13:11"/>He would certainly bring the full force of his power against you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That would make you very afraid of him!
<verse eid="JOB 13:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 13:12"/>You are quoting traditional sayings,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but they have no value in my situation.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Your arguments in support of your position
</para>
  <para style="q2">are very easy for someone to refute.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 13:13"/>So stop talking to me. Allow me to speak.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not care what God does to me as a result of what I say.
<verse eid="JOB 13:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 13:14"/>I will be risking my life.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will be taking the chance that God will execute me for what I say.
</para>
  <para style="q1">But I have good reason to do that.
<verse eid="JOB 13:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 13:15"/>Indeed, God might kill me {for starting to argue a case against him},
</para>
  <para style="q2">but I hope that he {would still judge me fairly after I die}.
</para>
  <para style="q1">So I am going to defend my behavior to him in person.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 13:16"/>I believe that arguing a case against God
</para>
  <para style="q2">will actually help convince God that I am innocent.
</para>
  <para style="q1">After all, no wicked person would dare to approach God personally.”

<verse eid="JOB 13:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 13:17"/>“God, listen very carefully to what I say.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Pay attention to me as I speak.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 13:18"/>Please pay careful attention because I will be defending myself
</para>
  <para style="q2">by telling you things I have thought carefully about.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I have examined myself,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I am convinced that I am innocent.
<verse eid="JOB 13:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 13:19"/>I do not believe that anyone could prove
</para>
  <para style="q2">that what I am going to say is false.
</para>
  <para style="q1">If I knew that it was false, I would not argue this case.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would accept dying {as my fair punishment}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 13:20"/>However, I must ask you to stop doing two things to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then I will be able to speak with you in person.
<verse eid="JOB 13:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 13:21"/>The first thing I ask is that you stop punishing me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The second thing I ask is that you stop scaring me.
<verse eid="JOB 13:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 13:22"/>{If you agree to stop punishing me and stop scaring me, then}
</para>
  <para style="q2">go ahead and start questioning me, and I will respond.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Or allow me to question you first, and then you respond to my questions.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 13:23"/>Tell me what I have done that is wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Tell me what sins I have committed.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Show me how I have disobeyed you.
<verse eid="JOB 13:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 13:24"/>I do not understand why you are avoiding me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not understand why you are treating me like your enemy.
<verse eid="JOB 13:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 13:25"/>I am as insignificant as a leaf that the wind blows,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so you do not need to punish me {as if I were a dangerous criminal}.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I am as unimportant as a bit of dry chaff,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so you do not need to chase me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 13:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 13:26"/>{From the way I am suffering, it appears that you are punishing me because}
</para>
  <para style="q2">you consider that I have done terrible things.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It seems that you are punishing me even for
</para>
  <para style="q2">the wrong things I did when I was young {and I did not know any better}.
<verse eid="JOB 13:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 13:27"/>It seems as if {you are judging me too strictly, as if} you had put chains on my feet {to keep me from going anywhere}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It feels as if you are watching everything that I do very closely.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if you will only let me do a few specific things.
<verse eid="JOB 13:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 13:28"/>{But you do not need to watch me so closely.}
</para>
  <para style="q1">I am a mere human being
</para>
  <para style="q2">with a body that is as fragile as rotten wood that is falling apart,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as fragile a piece of cloth that the larvae of moths are eating.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 13:28"/><verse eid="JOB 13:28"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 13"/>
  <chapter number="14" style="c" sid="JOB 14"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 14:1"/>Human beings live only a short time,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and during that time they experience much trouble.
<verse eid="JOB 14:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 14:2"/>People live brief lives, like flowers that appear quickly but then wither and fall to the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, people’s lives are temporary, like shadows that appear only for a short time.
<verse eid="JOB 14:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 14:3"/>God, you do not need to watch fragile humans so carefully.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You do not need to judge everything that I do.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 14:4"/>No one can make something that is acceptable to God out of something that is not acceptable to him.
<verse eid="JOB 14:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 14:5"/>You have decided how many days each human life will last.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, you have decided how many months each person will live.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one can live any longer than the time you have decided.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Because this is true,
<verse eid="JOB 14:5"/></para>
  <para style="q2"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 14:6"/>please stop watching people so closely.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Give them relief so that they can live out their brief lives in peace.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 14:7"/>Now even after people cut a tree down,
</para>
  <para style="q2">it is possible for it to start growing again and make new branches.
<verse eid="JOB 14:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 14:8"/>Its roots in the ground may be very old,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and its stump may be decaying,
<verse eid="JOB 14:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 14:9"/>but when rain falls on it,
</para>
  <para style="q2">it may revive and send up shoots just as a young plant does.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 14:10"/>But even the strongest people die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They grow weak and stop breathing,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then they are gone forever.
<verse eid="JOB 14:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 14:11"/>Just as water evaporates from a lake,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and just as a riverbed dries up,
<verse eid="JOB 14:11"/></para>
  <para style="q2"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 14:12"/>so people die and do not become alive again.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Until the heavens do not exist any longer,
</para>
  <para style="q2">people who die will not come back to life.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, no one will be able to make them alive again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 14:13"/>I wish that you, God, would put me safely in the place where dead people go and forget about me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That way I would not suffer any more until you were no longer angry with me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I wish that you would decide how much time I need to spend there
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then come and rescue me from that place.
<verse eid="JOB 14:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 14:14"/>I do not believe that we humans live again after we die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If I knew that I would live again, I would wait patiently throughout this difficult life.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would know that after I died, things would become different for me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 14:15"/>{After I had been in the place where dead people go for the time you had determined,} you would call for me, and I would answer you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You would be eager to see me, one of the creatures that you had made.
<verse eid="JOB 14:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 14:16"/>Yes, then you would protect me wherever I went,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and you would not be watching to see whether I sinned or not.
<verse eid="JOB 14:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 14:17"/>You would forgive the things I had done wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, it would be as if I had not done them at all.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 14:18"/>But just as mountains erode away and rocks tumble down slopes,
<verse eid="JOB 14:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 14:19"/>just as water wears away stones and floods wash away soil,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so you make all people die, even though they hope to keep living.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 14:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 14:20"/>You make us age the whole time we are living, and then we die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, you make our faces show that we are old and about to die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then you make us go to the place where dead people go.
<verse eid="JOB 14:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 14:21"/>When we die, we do not know whether our children will grow up and do things that will cause other people to honor them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If they do shameful things instead, we do not know that either.
<verse eid="JOB 14:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 14:22"/>When we die, we feel sorry for ourselves, but no one else feels sorry for us.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, we grieve for ourselves, but no one else grieves for us.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 14:22"/><verse eid="JOB 14:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 14"/>
  <chapter number="15" style="c" sid="JOB 15"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 15:1"/>Then Eliphaz, Job’s friend from the town of Teman, replied to what Job had said. Eliphaz told him,
<verse eid="JOB 15:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 15:2"/>“If you were truly wise, you would not have said such useless things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You have said a lot, but it has all been insignificant.
<verse eid="JOB 15:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 15:3"/>You should not be saying things that do not benefit anyone.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, you should not speak things that are not helpful.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 15:4"/>You are actually encouraging others not to respect God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, you are discouraging people from honoring him.
<verse eid="JOB 15:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 15:5"/>You are wicked, and that is why you say what you do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You are talking in the way that deceptive people talk.
<verse eid="JOB 15:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 15:6"/>Everything that you say shows that God should punish you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is not necessary for me to prove that.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 15:7"/>{You should not think that you know so much}
</para>
  <para style="q2">You were not the first person whom God created.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God did not make you when he first formed the world.
<verse eid="JOB 15:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 15:8"/>You do not listen as God makes his plans.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You should not think that you are the only person who is wise.
<verse eid="JOB 15:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 15:9"/>We know everything that you know.
</para>
  <para style="q2">We understand everything that you understand.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 15:10"/>My friends and I are saying the same things
</para>
  <para style="q2">that old, wise people say,
</para>
  <para style="q2">people who were born before your father was.
<verse eid="JOB 15:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 15:11"/>God wants to comfort you
</para>
  <para style="q2">and speak gently to you,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but you do not appreciate that.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 15:12"/>You should not be allowing yourself to become so emotional.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, you should not be allowing yourself to lose your temper.
<verse eid="JOB 15:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 15:13"/>You have become angry with God,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and so you are saying harsh things against him.
<verse eid="JOB 15:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 15:14"/>No human being can be sinless.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one on this earth can be completely righteous.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 15:15"/>God does not even trust his own angels {to do the right thing every time}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He does not consider the heavens to be completely pure.
<verse eid="JOB 15:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 15:16"/>So God certainly does not consider people to be pure who commit terrible wrongs,
</para>
  <para style="q2">who do evil deeds as often as they drink water.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 15:17"/>Job, I want to explain {why you are experiencing so much suffering}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Please listen to me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I have learned many things,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I want to explain them to you.
<verse eid="JOB 15:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 15:18"/>These are things that wise people have passed down from their ancestors.
</para>
  <para style="q2">These wise people have declared all these things openly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 15:19"/>God gave the land {of Edom} exclusively to my wise ancestors.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one came from another country and caused them to think wrongly about God.
<verse eid="JOB 15:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 15:20"/>Wicked people suffer great pain the whole time that they are alive.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God has determined that this will happen constantly to those who oppress others.
<verse eid="JOB 15:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 15:21"/>Wicked people constantly experience terrible things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They may become wealthy, but they will lose that wealth.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 15:22"/>Wicked people lose hope that their troubles will ever end.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are always afraid that they are going to die violently.
<verse eid="JOB 15:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 15:23"/>They have to go and search for food to eat. They do not know where to find it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They know that they will certainly experience disasters soon.
<verse eid="JOB 15:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 15:24"/>Wicked people are always afraid that bad things will happen to them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Their fear keeps them from taking actions that might protect them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if an opposing king had defeated them in a battle {and they had become helpless}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 15:25"/>These things happen to wicked people because they oppose God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They think they are strong enough to defeat Shaddai.
<verse eid="JOB 15:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 15:26"/>Wicked people stubbornly oppose God
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if they had a strong shield to protect themselves against God.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 15:27"/>But they are so fat that they would not be able to fight!
<verse eid="JOB 15:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 15:28"/>Wicked people have to live in cities that others have deserted.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They live in homes that others have abandoned.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those homes are about to collapse into a pile of ruins.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 15:29"/>Few wicked people become rich, and those who do become rich do not stay rich for very long.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, they do not acquire great wealth.
<verse eid="JOB 15:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 15:30"/>Wicked people will always be in trouble.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will be like trees whose branches burn up.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When God gives the command, they will die.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 15:31"/>Wicked people are often foolish and want to get things that are actually worthless.
</para>
  <para style="q2">In the end, all they have are those worthless things.
<verse eid="JOB 15:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 15:32"/>While they are still young, they will die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are like branches that have broken off a tree and dried up.
<verse eid="JOB 15:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 15:33"/>Wicked people are like vines whose grapes fall off before they are ripe.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are like olive trees whose blossoms fall off before they produce any fruit.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 15:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 15:34"/>Surely wicked people will not have any descendants.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those who took money from bribes will not have any money left in the end.
<verse eid="JOB 15:34"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="JOB 15:35"/>They plan to cause trouble and to do evil things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They continually think of ways to deceive other people.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 15:35"/><verse eid="JOB 15:35"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 15"/>
  <chapter number="16" style="c" sid="JOB 16"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 16:1"/>Then Job replied to Eliphaz and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 16:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 16:2"/>“I have heard many things like that before.
</para>
  <para style="q2">All of you, instead of helping me, are only causing me to feel more miserable.
<verse eid="JOB 16:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 16:3"/>I wish that you would stop saying these things that are not helping me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Eliphaz, I do not understand why you feel that you must keep replying to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 16:4"/>I could say the same things that you are saying
</para>
  <para style="q2">if you were the ones suffering instead of me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I could make great speeches to criticize you,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I could shake my head at you {to ridicule you}.
<verse eid="JOB 16:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 16:5"/>I would think that I was encouraging you by what I said.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, I would think that I was relieving you by speaking with you

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 16:6"/>But as it is, if I talk, I continue to suffer just as much,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and if I do not talk, that does not reduce my suffering either.
<verse eid="JOB 16:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 16:7"/>But now {I will speak to God}.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God, you have taken away all my strength,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and you have killed all of my children.
<verse eid="JOB 16:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 16:8"/>You have made me sick, and I have lost a lot of weight,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and people think that shows that I am a sinner.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They see how thin I am,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they think that proves that I am guilty.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 16:9"/>God is so angry with me that he has attacked me and hurt me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Now he is moving his teeth back and forth because he is still so angry with me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He is watching me closely {to see how he can attack me again}.
<verse eid="JOB 16:9"/><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 16:10"/>People look at me with their mouths wide open in order to humiliate me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They hit me in the face because they think I have done wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They crowd around me to threaten me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 16:11"/>God has allowed people who do not honor him to mistreat me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He has allowed wicked people to do whatever they want to me.
<verse eid="JOB 16:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 16:12"/>Previously, I was living peacefully,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but then God caused great trouble for me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It is as if he grabbed my neck and shook me to pieces.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if he set me up as a target

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 16:13"/>and people were surrounding me and shooting arrows at me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It is as if those arrows cut open my kidneys
</para>
  <para style="q2">and caused the gall from my liver to spill onto the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God is not having any mercy on me at all!
<verse eid="JOB 16:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 16:14"/>It is as if I were a wall that God was breaking down in many places.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if God were rushing at me like a soldier who was attacking his enemy.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 16:15"/>{I am grieving so deeply that} it is as if I have sewn rough mourning cloth right onto my skin!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I feel weak and humiliated.
<verse eid="JOB 16:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 16:16"/>My face is red because I have cried so much.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have dark circles around my eyes because I have not been sleeping well.
<verse eid="JOB 16:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 16:17"/>All this has happened to me even though I have not violently harmed anyone
</para>
  <para style="q2">and even though I pray sincerely to God.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 16:18"/>I feel as if I am the victim of a violent crime, and I want people to show concern about what has happened to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not want anyone to stop me while I am demanding that God act justly toward me.
<verse eid="JOB 16:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 16:19"/>But even now, I know that there is someone in heaven who will testify for me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He will confirm that what I have done is right.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 16:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 16:20"/>This person in heaven is my friend,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he will plead with God for me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">{At the same time, I will also plead with God
</para>
  <para style="q2">so earnestly} that I will weep.
<verse eid="JOB 16:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 16:21"/>My friend will plead with God for me in heaven
</para>
  <para style="q2">the same way one person pleads for another person here on earth.
<verse eid="JOB 16:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 16:22"/>{I want God to treat me justly now, while there is still time,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">because within a few years I will die.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 16:22"/><verse eid="JOB 16:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 16"/>
  <chapter number="17" style="c" sid="JOB 17"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 17:1"/>My time to live is almost over. I have no strength left.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am just about to die.
<verse eid="JOB 17:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 17:2"/>The people who are around me are continually making fun of me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have to watch them taunt me.

<verse eid="JOB 17:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 17:3"/>God, even though you would be trying my case,
</para>
  <para style="q2">please also guarantee to yourself that I will appear in court and behave as a defendant should.
</para>
  <para style="q2">There is certainly no one else who will do that for me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 17:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 17:4"/>My friends will not vouch for me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because you have prevented them from understanding that I am innocent.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Nevertheless, you will not prove them right by declaring me guilty.
<verse eid="JOB 17:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 17:5"/>{I know that you will treat me justly because you make sure that people act justly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">For example,} you punish people who give false testimony against their neighbors in exchange for a bribe.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You make their whole families suffer if they do that.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 17:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 17:6"/>But because God is punishing me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">people use my name to mean someone who was formerly prosperous but who experienced disaster because he was wicked.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People even spit in my face {to insult me}.
<verse eid="JOB 17:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 17:7"/>Because I am very sad, it has become hard for me to see.
</para>
  <para style="q2">My whole body has become so thin that it is as if I am not even here.
<verse eid="JOB 17:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 17:8"/>When people who are truly righteous see what has happened to me, it shocks them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It makes them want to punish people who really disobey God.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 17:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 17:9"/>Those who are truly righteous will continue to do what is right,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and those who are innocent will continue to become stronger.
<verse eid="JOB 17:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 17:10"/>But as for you friends of mine,
</para>
  <para style="q2">we could continue talking,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but I do not think that I will conclude that even one of you is wise.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 17:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 17:11"/>My time to live has almost ended.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not been able to do the things that I planned to do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I can no longer hope that the things I most desired will happen.
<verse eid="JOB 17:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 17:12"/>My friends say that the troubles I am experiencing are actually a good thing
</para>
  <para style="q2">{because God is using the troubles to correct me}.
</para>
  <para style="q1">My friends say that because my troubles have become so bad,
</para>
  <para style="q2">that means they must nearly be over.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 17:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 17:13"/>Suppose the only home I could hope to have was the place where dead people go.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Suppose I were going to stay in that dark place.
<verse eid="JOB 17:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 17:14"/>Suppose I considered the grave to be my family home,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and suppose I considered the worms that live in graves to be my family.
<verse eid="JOB 17:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 17:15"/>Then that would show that I truly do not have good things to hope for.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Everyone would recognize that I was not really hoping for anything.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 17:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 17:16"/>It would be as if everything I hoped for were going to the place where dead people go.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, it would be as if people were burying the things I have hoped for with me in a grave.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 17:16"/><verse eid="JOB 17:16"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 17"/>
  <chapter number="18" style="c" sid="JOB 18"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 18:1"/>Then Bildad, Job’s friend who was a Shuhite, responded to Job. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 18:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 18:2"/>“Please stop talking!
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you would stop talking and listen, we could tell you something.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 18:3"/>You seem to think that we are as stupid as cattle.
<verse eid="JOB 18:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 18:4"/>But by being so angry, you are only hurting yourself.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You seem to think that everyone should abandon the earth because life on earth is no longer worth living for anyone after what has happened to you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You seem to think that there should be a great earthquake {as a sign of what an awful thing has happened to you}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 18:5"/>But actually, what has happened to you is not momentous.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Wicked people are bound to lose their prosperity.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will not continue to enjoy happiness.
<verse eid="JOB 18:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 18:6"/>It will be as if the light went out in the tent in which they were living,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that they could not enjoy living there.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 18:7"/>Wicked people become weaker {because of the troubles they cause for themselves}.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They try to take advantage of other people,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but they hurt themselves instead.
<verse eid="JOB 18:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 18:8"/>Yes, it is as if they walk into a net that they do not realize is there
</para>
  <para style="q2">or fall into a pit that someone has concealed as a trap.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 18:9"/>It is as if their feet get caught in a trap
</para>
  <para style="q2">that does not let them go.
<verse eid="JOB 18:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 18:10"/>It is as if a loop of rope that someone has hidden on the ground
</para>
  <para style="q2">springs up and seizes them when they walk onto it.
<verse eid="JOB 18:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 18:11"/>Everywhere they go, things cause them to be terrified.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if those things are pursuing them and biting at their heels.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 18:12"/>They will become weak, as if they did not have enough food to eat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will always be at risk of disaster.
<verse eid="JOB 18:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 18:13"/>Diseases will spread all over their skin.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, a horrible disease will cause their bodies to decay.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 18:14"/>They have a secure life, but it ends suddenly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They die without being able to do anything to save their lives.
<verse eid="JOB 18:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 18:15"/>Then all of their possessions will belong to other people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Wicked people will have nothing left, as if God had burned up everything they had by making burning stones fall on it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 18:16"/>They will be lifeless, like trees whose roots have dried up
</para>
  <para style="q2">and whose branches have all withered.
<verse eid="JOB 18:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 18:17"/>No one on the earth will remember them anymore.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, no one anywhere in the world will recall what they were like.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 18:18"/>Suddenly wicked people will die and no longer be alive.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will no longer be able to live in this world.
<verse eid="JOB 18:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 18:19"/>They will have no children or grandchildren living in their people group after they die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will have no descendants living where they previously lived.
<verse eid="JOB 18:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 18:20"/>Anyone who hears about what happened to them
</para>
  <para style="q2">will be shocked.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 18:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 18:21"/>This is certainly what happens to unrighteous people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, this is what happens to people who do not honor God.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 18:21"/><verse eid="JOB 18:21"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 18"/>
  <chapter number="19" style="c" sid="JOB 19"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 19:1"/>Then Job replied to Bildad and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 19:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 19:2"/>“The three of you must stop hurting me
</para>
  <para style="q2">and discouraging me by saying that I am wicked!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 19:3"/>You have insulted me repeatedly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You should be ashamed for speaking so harshly to me!
<verse eid="JOB 19:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 19:4"/>Even if it were true that I had done wrong,
</para>
  <para style="q2">that would be my concern, not yours!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 19:5"/>If you truly think that you are better than I am,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and if you are claiming that I must be guilty because I am suffering,
<verse eid="JOB 19:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 19:6"/>you should realize that it is God who has caused me to suffer.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if he had a net and caught me in it, like an animal he wanted to trap.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 19:7"/>Listen to me: I protest that God is punishing me unfairly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but no one agrees with me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I cry for help, but no one, not even God, treats me fairly.
<verse eid="JOB 19:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 19:8"/>It is as if God has blocked my road
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that I can not go anywhere.
</para>
  <para style="q1">It is as if God has forced me
</para>
  <para style="q2">to try to find my way in total darkness.
<verse eid="JOB 19:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 19:9"/>He has taken away my good reputation.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if he has taken a crown of authority off my head.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 19:10"/>He is ruining everything that I have.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I no longer hope to experience good things in this life.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I expect that I will soon die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if I were a tree that he had pulled completely out of the ground.
<verse eid="JOB 19:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 19:11"/>He attacks me because he is very angry with me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He treats me as if I were his enemy.
<verse eid="JOB 19:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 19:12"/>It is as if he were sending an army against me
</para>
  <para style="q2">that has surrounded me and is getting ready to destroy me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 19:13"/>God has caused my family to abandon me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those who know me now act like strangers toward me.
<verse eid="JOB 19:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 19:14"/>My relatives have left me
</para>
  <para style="q2">and my good friends ignore me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 19:15"/>People who were guests in my house and even my female servants
</para>
  <para style="q2">act as if they do not recognize me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They treat me like a foreigner whom they do not know.
<verse eid="JOB 19:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 19:16"/>When I order my servants to come, they do not obey.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have to beg my servants to help me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 19:17"/>My wife stays away from me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even though I was a good father to our children.
<verse eid="JOB 19:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 19:18"/>Even young children disrespect me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When I stand up to talk, they laugh at me.
<verse eid="JOB 19:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 19:19"/>All of my intimate friends now detest me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The people I love are hostile to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 19:20"/>I have become so thin that my bones are visible beneath my skin;
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am barely alive.
<verse eid="JOB 19:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 19:21"/>I plead with you, my three friends, pity me
</para>
  <para style="q2">because God is making me suffer greatly.
<verse eid="JOB 19:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 19:22"/>You should not be making me suffer as well,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if you were God and had to punish sin.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You seem to think that you need to keep accusing me of doing things wrong!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 19:23"/>I wish that someone would now write what I have been saying
</para>
  <para style="q2">in a book {so that people could read it}.
<verse eid="JOB 19:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 19:24"/>{Or else,} I wish that someone would carve what I have said onto a rock with a chisel
</para>
  <para style="q2">and highlight it with lead so that people could always read it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 19:25"/>But I know that there is someone who will defend me
</para>
  <para style="q2">and that some day he will appear here on the earth and declare me innocent.

<verse eid="JOB 19:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 19:26"/>Even after I die and people bury me and my body decays,
</para>
  <para style="q2">still, in my body I will see God.
<verse eid="JOB 19:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 19:27"/>I will see him myself!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, I will see him personally!
</para>
  <para style="q2">My emotions overwhelm me as I think about that!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 19:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 19:28"/>But if you three men still think that you need to accuse me of doing wrong
</para>
  <para style="q2">because you believe I am responsible for the sufferings I am experiencing,
<verse eid="JOB 19:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 19:29"/>then you should fear that God will punish you,
</para>
  <para style="q1">because God becomes angry with unfair people like you and he punishes them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When that happens, you will know that God punishes people fairly.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 19:29"/><verse eid="JOB 19:29"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 19"/>
  <chapter number="20" style="c" sid="JOB 20"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 20:1"/>Then Zophar, Job’s friend from the town of Naamah, responded to Job. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 20:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 20:2"/>“What you have said troubles me greatly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so I want to reply to you right away.
<verse eid="JOB 20:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 20:3"/>You have insulted me by what you have said.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But I have had a good idea about how I should respond to you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 20:4"/>People have known this for a long time,
</para>
  <para style="q2">for as long as they have existed:
<verse eid="JOB 20:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 20:5"/>Wicked people are only briefly successful;
</para>
  <para style="q2">people who refuse to honor God are happy only for a short time.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 20:6"/>They may seem for a while to be very great
</para>
  <para style="q2">and very powerful,
<verse eid="JOB 20:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 20:7"/>but they will disappear forever like refuse that people throw away.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People who knew them will wonder where they have gone.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 20:8"/>They will disappear the way a dream ends abruptly when a person wakes up.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will no longer exist.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Yes, they will vanish just like the dreams that people have at night.
<verse eid="JOB 20:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 20:9"/>Those who once saw those wicked people will never see them again.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those wicked people will no longer live where they once lived.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 20:10"/>They will have to return the valuable things that they stole from poor people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If they die before they can do that, their children will have to do it.
<verse eid="JOB 20:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 20:11"/>Wicked people will die before they grow old
</para>
  <para style="q2">and others will bury them in the ground.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 20:12"/>For wicked people, doing wrong things is like having sweet food in their mouths
</para>
  <para style="q2">that they want to continue tasting.
<verse eid="JOB 20:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 20:13"/>They do not want to stop doing wrong things,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if those things were delicious food that they enjoyed eating.
<verse eid="JOB 20:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 20:14"/>{But they will regret doing those wrong things.}
</para>
  <para style="q1">They will feel like people who eat what they should not eat
</para>
  <para style="q2">and get a badly upset stomach
</para>
  <para style="q2">that feels as if it is full of poison.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 20:15"/>Wicked people try to get as much money as they can but they do not keep it,
</para>
  <para style="q2">just as people who eat too much throw up the food that they ate.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God makes sure that they lose their wealth.
<verse eid="JOB 20:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 20:16"/>A person who does evil deeds will die as a result,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as certainly as the bite of a poisonous snake kills a person.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 20:17"/>A wicked person will not experience the abundant blessings
</para>
  <para style="q2">that flow like a stream from God.
<verse eid="JOB 20:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 20:18"/>Wicked people will have to give back the things that they acquired dishonestly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They will not be able to continue using those things.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They will not have the opportunity to enjoy the things they got by doing business,
<verse eid="JOB 20:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 20:19"/>because they oppressed poor people and refused to help them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they took other people’s houses by cheating them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 20:20"/>No matter how much they got, they still remained greedy,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and as a result, they lost the things that they enjoyed having.
<verse eid="JOB 20:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 20:21"/>When they finish eating their food, there is never anything left over,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because they greedily eat it all.
</para>
  <para style="q1">That is why they do not remain wealthy.
<verse eid="JOB 20:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 20:22"/>While they are still very wealthy,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they will suddenly experience trouble.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Many people will be hostile to them and hurt them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 20:23"/>Even as wicked people are enjoying luxuries,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will show that he is very angry with them.
</para>
  <para style="q1">He will punish them with great suffering,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even as they are indulging themselves.

<verse eid="JOB 20:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 20:24"/>Even as they are trying to escape from smaller punishments,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they will experience greater punishments.
<verse eid="JOB 20:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 20:25"/>It will be as if God had shot an arrow through a wicked person’s body
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he had to pull it out of his back.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But the shiny point of the arrow would have cut a gash in his liver,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he would know that he was going to die.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 20:26"/>God has decided that he will destroy the valuable possessions that wicked people have.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It will be as if a fire that God starts will burn them up.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, God will destroy every last thing that they have in their homes.
<verse eid="JOB 20:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 20:27"/>The sins that those wicked people have committed will become apparent.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If the sky could speak, it would tell all the wrong things it saw them do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If the earth could speak, it would also be a witness against them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 20:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 20:28"/>On the day when God punishes people,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he will take away all the possessions of wicked people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It will be as if a flood had washed them away.
<verse eid="JOB 20:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 20:29"/>That is what God will do to wicked people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That is what God has decreed will happen to them.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 20:29"/><verse eid="JOB 20:29"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 20"/>
  <chapter number="21" style="c" sid="JOB 21"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 21:1"/>Then Job replied to Zophar and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 21:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 21:2"/>“Please listen carefully to what I say.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That is something that you can do to comfort me.
<verse eid="JOB 21:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 21:3"/>Be patient with me, and allow me to speak.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then, after I have finished speaking,
</para>
  <para style="q2">you can continue to make fun of me if you wish{, although I hope you will not}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 21:4"/>I am not protesting to people about my suffering, I am protesting to God!
</para>
  <para style="q2">And so it is certainly acceptable for me to be impatient!
<verse eid="JOB 21:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 21:5"/>If you consider my situation, it will shock you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That should make you put your hands over your mouths {and say no more}!
<verse eid="JOB 21:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 21:6"/>When I think about what has happened to me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am frightened and my entire body shakes.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 21:7"/>I do not understand why wicked people live long lives
</para>
  <para style="q2">and become very powerful.
<verse eid="JOB 21:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 21:8"/>They live to see their children grow up
</para>
  <para style="q2">and start their own households nearby.
<verse eid="JOB 21:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 21:9"/>Wicked people live safely in their homes without being afraid.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does not punish them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 21:10"/>Their bulls mate with their cows successfully.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Their cows give birth to calves. They do not miscarry.
<verse eid="JOB 21:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 21:11"/>Wicked people are able to send their young children outside to play {without worrying about what will happen to them}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Their children dance playfully.
<verse eid="JOB 21:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 21:12"/>The children play tambourines and lyres {as they sing},
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they also play flutes happily.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 21:13"/>Wicked people enjoy having good things while they are alive,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then they die peacefully and go to the place where dead people go.
<verse eid="JOB 21:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 21:14"/>While they are alive, they say to God, ‘Leave us alone;
</para>
  <para style="q2">we do not care about how you want us to live our lives!’
<verse eid="JOB 21:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 21:15"/>Those wicked people also say, ‘It does not matter whether we worship Shaddai.
</para>
  <para style="q2">We would not get any advantage by praying to him.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 21:16"/>Wicked people wrongly think that they have become prosperous because of what they have done.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I want to think completely differently from the way they think.
<verse eid="JOB 21:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 21:17"/>It often happens that wicked people die
</para>
  <para style="q2">without experiencing any disasters.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not think that God actually becomes angry with them and punishes them.
<verse eid="JOB 21:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 21:18"/>God does not destroy them like straw that the wind blows away.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is never as if a whirlwind carries them off.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 21:19"/>You say, ‘When people have committed sins,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God remembers and punishes their children for those sins.’
</para>
  <para style="q1">I say that God should punish the people who sin, not their children.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That way sinners will know that God is punishing them for their own sins.
<verse eid="JOB 21:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 21:20"/>Wicked people should have the experience of God destroying them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They should know what it is like for Shaddai to punish them in his wrath.
<verse eid="JOB 21:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 21:21"/>After all, once wicked people die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they are no longer concerned for their families who are still alive.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 21:22"/>Since God judges everyone, even those who are in heaven,
</para>
  <para style="q2">no one can teach him anything.
<verse eid="JOB 21:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 21:23"/>Some people die while they are still very healthy.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They die when their lives are peaceful and they are not afraid that bad things will happen to them.
<verse eid="JOB 21:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 21:24"/>Their bodies are fat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Their bones are strong.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 21:25"/>Other people are very miserable when they die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They die before good things have happened to them.
<verse eid="JOB 21:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 21:26"/>Both kinds of people die, and other people bury them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Their bodies all decompose.
</para>
  <para style="q1">{Everyone dies, so it is clear that dying is not always the punishment for being wicked.}

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 21:27"/>Listen, I know what you three are thinking about me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You are wrongly applying to me things that are true about wicked people.
<verse eid="JOB 21:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 21:28"/>I know you are wrong because you say, ‘God does not allow evil rulers to keep living!
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does not allow wicked people to keep living!’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 21:29"/>If you want to know what life on earth is really like, you should ask people who travel much {and see many things}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will have to admit that the things they see and describe show what life is really like.
<verse eid="JOB 21:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 21:30"/>{Travelers will report} that wicked people do not suffer when there are great disasters.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{They will report} that wicked people escape from God’s punishment.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 21:31"/>No one dares to accuse wicked people openly of doing wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one punishes them for the evil things they have done.
<verse eid="JOB 21:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 21:32"/>When wicked people die, others carry their bodies to their graves in a solemn procession.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then people guard their graves to protect them.
<verse eid="JOB 21:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 21:33"/>It would please wicked people to see how others build mounds over their graves to honor them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It would also please them to see how many people take part in their funeral processions.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Many people walk in front of their bodies and many others walk behind them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 21:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 21:34"/>Since all of this is true about wicked people, what you have been telling me does not make sense, and it has not comforted me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You have been trying to answer me by telling me things that are not true!”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 21:34"/><verse eid="JOB 21:34"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 21"/>
  <chapter number="22" style="c" sid="JOB 22"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 22:1"/>Then Eliphaz, Job’s friend from the town of Teman, replied to what Job had said. Eliphaz told him,
<verse eid="JOB 22:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 22:2"/>“No one can do anything to benefit God!
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, people can only do things to benefit themselves, and only if they are wise.
<verse eid="JOB 22:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 22:3"/>It does not give Shaddai any extra pleasure if you live in the way you are supposed to live anyway.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It does not make him better off if you improve the way you are living.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 22:4"/>God would not be punishing you in this way if you truly honored him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God would not judge you to be guilty for doing that.
<verse eid="JOB 22:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 22:5"/>No, God must be punishing you because you are very wicked.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must have done a great number of evil things.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 22:6"/>You must have unfairly forced poor people to give you things to guarantee that they would pay money back to you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must have taken the cloaks that they needed to keep them warm.
<verse eid="JOB 22:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 22:7"/>You must not have given water to people who were thirsty.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must not have given food to people who were hungry.
<verse eid="JOB 22:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 22:8"/>You must have refused to help others even though you were a wealthy man who owned a lot of land.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must have enjoyed a life of privilege {without being concerned for others}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 22:9"/>When widows came to you for help, you must have sent them away without giving them anything.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must have oppressed orphans.
<verse eid="JOB 22:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 22:10"/>Because you did all those things, now you are having difficulties in many different areas of your life.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Things are happening all of a sudden that scare you.
<verse eid="JOB 22:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 22:11"/>And so you do not know what to do, as if it had become very dark and you could not see anything.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You cannot solve your problems; it is as if you were drowning in a flood of water.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 22:12"/>But God lives high up in the heavens.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He looks at the earth from a place that is even higher than the stars.
<verse eid="JOB 22:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 22:13"/>So you should not say, ‘God does not know what people are doing.’
</para>
  <para style="q2">You should not say, ‘Dark clouds keep him from seeing us, so he cannot judge us.’
<verse eid="JOB 22:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 22:14"/>You should not say, ‘Since he walks on the dome that covers the sky,
</para>
  <para style="q2">where there are thick clouds around him, and he can not see what we do.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 22:15"/>You should not continue to conduct your life
</para>
  <para style="q2">in the way that evil people have done for many years.
<verse eid="JOB 22:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 22:16"/>God makes evil people die suddenly while they are still young.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They go away completely, as if a flood had swept them away.
<verse eid="JOB 22:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 22:17"/>Those evil people told God, ‘Leave us alone!’
</para>
  <para style="q2">They also said defiantly, ‘Shaddai can do nothing to harm us!’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 22:18"/>Even so, God mercifully gave them many things to enjoy.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Nevertheless, I want to think completely differently from the way evil people think.
<verse eid="JOB 22:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 22:19"/>When good people see that God punishes evil people, they are glad.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People who have not done wrong say that those evil people are getting what they deserve.
<verse eid="JOB 22:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 22:20"/>Those good people say, ‘Now God has destroyed our enemies!
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if a fire has burned up everything they owned.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 22:21"/>So, Job, I urge you to ask God to forgive you. Restore your relationship with him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you do that, good things will happen to you.
<verse eid="JOB 22:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 22:22"/>Do pay attention to what God teaches you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Meditate on what he says.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 22:23"/>If you come humbly to Shaddai, he will restore you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{God will certainly do that} if you stop doing the evil things that you have been doing.
<verse eid="JOB 22:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 22:24"/>Stop trusting in your wealth.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It should be as if you are throwing away your gold,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even the finest gold you have, onto the dirt and rocks.
<verse eid="JOB 22:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 22:25"/>If you do that, then Shaddai will be more precious to you than your gold and your silver ever were.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 22:26"/>If you do that, then your relationship with Shaddai will make you very happy.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will be able to approach God confidently.
<verse eid="JOB 22:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 22:27"/>When you pray to God, he will do what you ask him to do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will have many things to praise God for.
<verse eid="JOB 22:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 22:28"/>The things that you decide to do will be successful.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It will be as if a light were shining on the road in front of you so that you knew just where to walk.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 22:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 22:29"/>People may cause great difficulties for you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But when you pray and humbly ask God to help you,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he will save you.
<verse eid="JOB 22:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 22:30"/>God will even help people who have done wrong things if you pray for them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will answer your prayer because you will not have done wrong things.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 22:30"/><verse eid="JOB 22:30"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 22"/>
  <chapter number="23" style="c" sid="JOB 23"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 23:1"/>Then Job replied to Eliphaz and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 23:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 23:2"/>“I must still complain about many distressing things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">There is much more that I could have protested about.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 23:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 23:3"/>I wish that I knew where I could find God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{If I knew that,} I would go to the place where he lives.
<verse eid="JOB 23:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 23:4"/>I would present much evidence to God that I am innocent.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would offer many arguments to prove that.
<verse eid="JOB 23:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 23:5"/>That way I would know what God would say
</para>
  <para style="q2">in reply to everything I would say to him.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 23:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 23:6"/>He would not use his great power to defeat me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He would listen to me fairly.
<verse eid="JOB 23:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 23:7"/>I am an honest man, so if I met with God personally, I would be able to discuss things with him reasonably.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{If we could do that,} he would declare that I am innocent.
</para>
  <para style="q2">After that, he would not punish me again as if I had sinned.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 23:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 23:8"/>{But I do not know where to find God.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">If I went to the east, he would not be there.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If I went to the west, I would not see him there.
<verse eid="JOB 23:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 23:9"/>He makes natural things happen beautifully in the north,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but no one there sees him doing those things.
</para>
  <para style="q1">He must also go to the south,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but no one sees him there either.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 23:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 23:10"/>Still, he knows how I have conducted my life.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When he has finished testing me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he will see that I am as pure as gold whose impurities fire has burned out.
<verse eid="JOB 23:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 23:11"/>I have lived faithfully in the manner that God wants people to live.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not stopped living that way.
<verse eid="JOB 23:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 23:12"/>I have always obeyed the commandments that he has given.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would rather go without food than disobey what God has told us to do.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 23:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 23:13"/>But God seems to have made up his mind that I am a sinner,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and no one can make God change his mind.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Whatever he wants to do, he does.
<verse eid="JOB 23:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 23:14"/>So it seems that God will keep on punishing me until I die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If my present troubles do not kill me, I am sure that God will cause many more troubles for me!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 23:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 23:15"/>So the thought of coming into his presence greatly frightens me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When I think about what he can do, that makes me very afraid of him.
<verse eid="JOB 23:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 23:16"/>I am afraid because I am no longer brave after what God has done to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Shaddai has made me very frightened.

<verse eid="JOB 23:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 23:17"/>I have lived to experience terrible things, and that is why I am so frightened.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I can only imagine that the future will be just as bad as the present.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 23:17"/><verse eid="JOB 23:17"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 23"/>
  <chapter number="24" style="c" sid="JOB 24"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 24:1"/>Shaddai should set times when he judges evil people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The people who obey God never seem to see him judge evil people.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 24:2"/>Some evil people remove boundary markers so that they can steal other people’s land.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Some evil people steal other people’s sheep and put them in with their own sheep.
<verse eid="JOB 24:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 24:3"/>Some evil people take away donkeys that belong to children who have no fathers to protect them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They make loans to widows, and then they take oxen that belong to them to guarantee that the widows will pay back the money.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{But without the oxen, the widows cannot grow crops, so they cannot repay the loans, and the evil people keep the oxen.}
<verse eid="JOB 24:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 24:4"/>Poor people do not want evil people to know where they are, so they do not walk on the main roads.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Poor people stay in hiding places in order to avoid evil people.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 24:5"/>In fact, poor people have to search in the desert plain for food to feed their children,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if they were wild donkeys.
<verse eid="JOB 24:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 24:6"/>The poor people pick up leftover grain in other people’s fields.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They gather leftover grapes from vineyards that evil people own.
<verse eid="JOB 24:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 24:7"/>During the night they do not have thick garments to cover their bodies,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so they are not able to stay warm.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 24:8"/>{Poor people have no homes, so} when rain showers come from the mountains, they get very wet.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They have to huddle under rock ledges to shelter themselves from the rain.
<verse eid="JOB 24:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 24:9"/>Some evil people even take babies away from their mothers while they are nursing!
</para>
  <para style="q2">They take the babies as a guarantee that their mothers will repay money that they have lent them.
<verse eid="JOB 24:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 24:10"/>Poor people do not have adequate clothing.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They also do not have adequate food,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even though they carry around bundles of grain because they work to bring in other people’s harvests.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 24:11"/>Evil people hire poor people to make oil from olives that grow on olive trees on their property.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The evil people also hire poor people to tread on grapes that grow on their vines to make juice for wine.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But they do not allow the poor people to drink any of the wine when they are thirsty.
<verse eid="JOB 24:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 24:12"/>In the cities, poor people groan because they are suffering.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People whom evil people have wounded cry out to God for help.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But God does not seem to care about what the evil people are doing to them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 24:13"/>These evil people are like others who do not obey God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They do not try to understand what God wants or to do it.
<verse eid="JOB 24:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 24:14"/>Murderers go out in the dim light of morning so they can kill vulnerable people {without being caught}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are like thieves who steal things in the dark of night {in order to avoid being caught}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 24:15"/>Men who want to commit adultery watch for evening to come.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They say, ‘I am going to make sure that no one recognizes me,’ and they disguise themselves.
<verse eid="JOB 24:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 24:16"/>During the night, robbers find ways to get into other people’s houses and steal things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But during the day, they stay in their own homes.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They do not like being out where it is light.
<verse eid="JOB 24:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 24:17"/>These evil people all stay at home during the day because morning is as scary for them as the dark of night is for other people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They stay up at night because they like being awake at night when things happen that terrify others.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 24:18"/>However, those wicked people will not live long lives.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will keep anything from growing on the land that they own.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one will go and work in their vineyards.
<verse eid="JOB 24:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 24:19"/>Just as water from melting snow dries up when it is hot and there is no rain,
</para>
  <para style="q2">those evil people will go to the place where dead people go.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 24:20"/>When they die, maggots will eat their corpses,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and no one will remember them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even their mothers will forget about them!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those wicked people will be like trees that fall down and rot.

<verse eid="JOB 24:20"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 24:21"/>Those evil people mistreat women who do not have children to care for them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those evil people never help widows.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 24:22"/>But God, by his power, gets rid of people who hurt others because they are stronger than those others.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When it is clear that God is going to punish those evil people, they know that they are going to die.
<verse eid="JOB 24:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 24:23"/>God allows them to think that they are secure, and so they do not worry,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but God is carefully watching how they live.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 24:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 24:24"/>Those evil people may prosper for a little while,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but suddenly they will be gone.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God will show that they were not greater than others.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God will make them die like all other people.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Then they will be like stalks of grain that farmers have cut off.
<verse eid="JOB 24:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 24:25"/>What I have said is certainly true.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Now that I have explained it,
</para>
  <para style="q2">there is no one who can show that I am a liar
</para>
  <para style="q2">and prove me wrong.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 24:25"/><verse eid="JOB 24:25"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 24"/>
  <chapter number="25" style="c" sid="JOB 25"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 25:1"/>Then Bildad, Job’s friend who was a Shuhite, responded to Job. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 25:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 25:2"/>“God is very powerful.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Everyone should honor him greatly.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God causes everything to be orderly high up in heaven.
<verse eid="JOB 25:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 25:3"/>No one can count all the stars in the sky.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When the sun rises, it shines on everyone.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 25:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 25:4"/>Since God is so great, no person can be righteous compared with him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No human being is innocent by his standards.
<verse eid="JOB 25:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 25:5"/>Consider this: God is so glorious that he does not even consider the full moon to be bright!
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even the white light of the stars does not seem pure to him.
<verse eid="JOB 25:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 25:6"/>So God would certainly not consider mortal humans to be pure!”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 25:6"/><verse eid="JOB 25:6"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 25"/>
  <chapter number="26" style="c" sid="JOB 26"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 26:1"/>Then Job replied to Bildad and the others. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 26:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 26:2"/>“I am struggling, but you have not really helped me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am not able to overcome my difficulties, but you have not rescued me.
<verse eid="JOB 26:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 26:3"/>I need good advice, but you have not given me any.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You have said many things, but they have not been helpful.

<verse eid="JOB 26:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 26:4"/>I suppose you think that God helped you tell me important things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But God was not really speaking through you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 26:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 26:5"/>{Let me tell you how great God really is.}
</para>
  <para style="q1">The spirits of dead people tremble with fear at God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are deep in the earth,
</para>
  <para style="q2">below the waters where the sea creatures live.
<verse eid="JOB 26:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 26:6"/>God knows all about those who are in the place where dead people go.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Nothing prevents God from seeing what is there.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 26:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 26:7"/>God created the dome of the sky to make an orderly world.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God created the dry land where previously there was nowhere for people to live.
<verse eid="JOB 26:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 26:8"/>God uses clouds as containers for rainwater,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he prevents that water from bursting the clouds.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 26:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 26:9"/>God can make clouds so thick that even the bright moon cannot shine through them.
<verse eid="JOB 26:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 26:10"/>God has set the horizon in place as the outer edge of the oceans.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The horizon is at the place where the sky meets the earth.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 26:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 26:11"/>Sometimes even the mountains that hold up the sky shake,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if God were scolding them and they were afraid.
<verse eid="JOB 26:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 26:12"/>God used his power to restrain the oceans;
</para>
  <para style="q2">by his skill he destroyed Rahab, the huge sea monster.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 26:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 26:13"/>God can make the sky clear by blowing away the clouds.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{God brings order everywhere in creation} by overcoming the forces of chaos.
<verse eid="JOB 26:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 26:14"/>But I have described only a little bit of what God does.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if we can only hear someone whisper about him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When we hear mighty thunder, we realize that God is far too great for us to understand.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 26:14"/><verse eid="JOB 26:14"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 26"/>
  <chapter number="27" style="c" sid="JOB 27"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 27:1"/>Job kept speaking to his three friends. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 27:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 27:2"/>“I will promise you something as surely as God exists, even though he has treated me unfairly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will promise you something as surely as Shaddai exists, even though he has made my life so unpleasant.
<verse eid="JOB 27:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 27:3"/>I will insist on it for as long as I am alive.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, for as long as I am still breathing, I will insist on it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 27:4"/>I promise that I will not say the wrong thing!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I promise that I will not say anything to deceive anyone!
<verse eid="JOB 27:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 27:5"/>And so I will never say that what you three have said about me is true.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Until the day that I die, I will insist that I have been living as God wants people to live.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 27:6"/>I will insist that I am innocent. I will never say anything different.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That way, for as long as I live, I will not feel badly about saying something that was not true.
<verse eid="JOB 27:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 27:7"/>I want to be just the opposite of an evil person.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I want to disagree with the way anyone who is a bad person is living.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 27:8"/>I want to be the opposite of an evil person because
</para>
  <para style="q2">when God decides it is time for that person to die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he can only expect that God will then punish him for the wrong things that he has done.
<verse eid="JOB 27:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 27:9"/>When evil people are in trouble,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does not rescue them when they pray for help.
<verse eid="JOB 27:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 27:10"/>Evil people are not happy about what Shaddai does.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They do not speak with God in prayer in good times as well as in bad times.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 27:11"/>I need to teach you three something
</para>
  <para style="q2">about how God actually deals with the people of this world.
<verse eid="JOB 27:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 27:12"/>You three have certainly seen for yourselves how God treats people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So you should not have been telling me things that have made so little sense.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 27:13"/>I will tell you how God punishes evil people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">This is what Shaddai does to those who mistreat others:
<verse eid="JOB 27:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 27:14"/>Even if they have many children, those children will die in wars,
</para>
  <para style="q2">or they will die because they do not have enough food to eat.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 27:15"/>Any children who are still alive after their evil parents die will perish from diseases.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Not even the wife of an evil man will mourn for him if he dies.
<verse eid="JOB 27:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 27:16"/>Sometimes evil people accumulate a large amount of money
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they acquire many valuable articles of clothing.
<verse eid="JOB 27:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 27:17"/>But even if evil people become rich, they will die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then righteous people will get their clothes.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Honest people will get their money and share it with one another.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 27:18"/>The houses that they build are as weak as spider webs.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Their houses are flimsy, like the huts that watchmen live in while they guard people’s fields.
<verse eid="JOB 27:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 27:19"/>God makes evil people lose their money.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if they are rich when they go to bed one night,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but the next morning they wake up to find that their money is all gone.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 27:20"/>When evil people realize that God is punishing them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they become very afraid, as if those fears were water in which they were drowning.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God soon destroys the evil people, as if a whirlwind carried them away in the night.
<verse eid="JOB 27:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 27:21"/>It is as if a windstorm from the desert picks them up and carries them away from their homes.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one ever sees them again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 27:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 27:22"/>It is as if a wind like that blows steadily against them
</para>
  <para style="q2">while they are running away, trying to escape from its force.
<verse eid="JOB 27:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 27:23"/>It is as if such a wind were clapping its hands at them to mock them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if such a wind blew them out of their houses and stayed there laughing at them.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 27:23"/><verse eid="JOB 27:23"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 27"/>
  <chapter number="28" style="c" sid="JOB 28"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 28:1"/>There certainly are places where people dig to find silver.
</para>
  <para style="q2">There certainly are places where people refine gold that they have dug from the ground.
<verse eid="JOB 28:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 28:2"/>People get iron from ore that they dig out of the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They get copper by heating and melting ore that contains it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 28:3"/>People use lamps so that they can see while they work far down under the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They search for ore in many places where it is very dark.
<verse eid="JOB 28:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 28:4"/>People dig shafts deep into the ground in places that are far from where other people live,
</para>
  <para style="q2">As they work underground, the people who walk above them are not aware of them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People have to climb down ropes in order to descend into the deep shafts they have dug.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 28:5"/>Food grows on the surface of the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But deep below the ground, miners make fires to break apart the rocks.
<verse eid="JOB 28:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 28:6"/>The stones that people dig from under the ground contain sapphires.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The dirt they dig up contains bits of gold.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 28:7"/>Even birds that have very good eyesight can not see deep down into these mines.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even birds such as falcons have not looked into them.
<verse eid="JOB 28:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 28:8"/>Strong beasts can go wherever they want, but they have never gone to those places.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Not even lions have gone there.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 28:9"/>Miners dig through even very hard rock.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as though they are turning mountains upside down in order to get at the ore that is at the bases of them.
<verse eid="JOB 28:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 28:10"/>Miners cut tunnels through rocks
</para>
  <para style="q2">in order to find many precious things.
<verse eid="JOB 28:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 28:11"/>They dam up small streams to stop the water from flowing.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That allows them to find things that were hidden beneath the water of the streams.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 28:12"/>{It takes great effort to find valuable ore.} It is even more difficult to obtain wisdom.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, it is very difficult to obtain understanding.
<verse eid="JOB 28:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 28:13"/>Humans do not know how to obtain wisdom.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Wisdom is not available on earth, where people live.
<verse eid="JOB 28:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 28:14"/>It is as though the ocean depths and the water of the seas said,
</para>
  <para style="q2">‘There is no wisdom here!’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 28:15"/>People can not buy wisdom
</para>
  <para style="q2">by paying for it with gold or silver.
<verse eid="JOB 28:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 28:16"/>Wisdom is worth much more than fine gold that comes from the land of Ophir.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{Wisdom is worth much more} than very valuable stones.
<verse eid="JOB 28:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 28:17"/>Wisdom is worth much more than gold or beautiful quartz.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is more expensive than an article made of pure gold.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 28:18"/>Wisdom is worth more than coral or jasper.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The price of wisdom is higher than the price of rubies.
<verse eid="JOB 28:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 28:19"/>Wisdom is worth more than topaz from the land of Cush.
</para>
  <para style="q2">One can not express the value of wisdom even in terms of gold.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 28:20"/>So, then, wisdom is very difficult to obtain;
</para>
  <para style="q2">understanding is indeed a very valuable thing to get.
<verse eid="JOB 28:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 28:21"/>Living creatures are not able to see it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Not even the birds can see it while they are up in the sky.
<verse eid="JOB 28:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 28:22"/>Even after people die, they do not have a better experience of wisdom
</para>
  <para style="q2">in the different place to which they go.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 28:23"/>God is the only one who knows how people can become wise.
<verse eid="JOB 28:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 28:24"/>God knows this because he can see everything on earth, even in its most remote places.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God can see everything that is below the sky.
<verse eid="JOB 28:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 28:25"/>He decided how strongly the winds should blow
</para>
  <para style="q2">and how much rain should be in the clouds.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 28:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 28:26"/>God decided where rain should fall
</para>
  <para style="q2">and what path lightning should take from the clouds down to the ground.
<verse eid="JOB 28:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 28:27"/>At that time, he identified wisdom and recognized that it was very valuable.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He was delighted by its capabilities and made it a permanent part of his creation.
<verse eid="JOB 28:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 28:28"/>Then God told human beings, ‘Listen! You will become wise to the extent that you respect me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you want to understand what you should do, you must first decide that you will not do anything wicked.’”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 28:28"/><verse eid="JOB 28:28"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 28"/>
  <chapter number="29" style="c" sid="JOB 29"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 29:1"/>Job kept speaking to his three friends. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 29:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 29:2"/>“I wish that I could be as I was previously
</para>
  <para style="q2">during the time when God was taking care of me.
<verse eid="JOB 29:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 29:3"/>During that time, it was if God was shining a light on me
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that I could see where I was going even in the darkness.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 29:4"/>At that time I was young and strong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Because God was my friend, he protected where I lived.
<verse eid="JOB 29:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 29:5"/>Shaddai was still present with me during that time
</para>
  <para style="q2">when all my children were still alive and living near me.
<verse eid="JOB 29:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 29:6"/>My herds provided me with plenty of milk.
</para>
  <para style="q2">My olive trees provided me with plenty of oil.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 29:7"/>I used to go to the place where the elders gathered at the city gate.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The people had reserved a seat for me there, and I would sit in it.
<verse eid="JOB 29:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 29:8"/>When young men saw me, they stepped aside respectfully.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When older men saw me, they stood up respectfully.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 29:9"/>The leaders of the people used to stop talking when I arrived.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They made sure they did not say anything{, because they knew it would not be as good as what I was going to say}.
<verse eid="JOB 29:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 29:10"/>Even the most important leaders became quiet.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It was as if they were not even able to speak.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 29:11"/>When people heard what I had to say,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they said good things about me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They recognized that I had given wise counsel, and they approved of me
<verse eid="JOB 29:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 29:12"/>{They approved of me} because I had helped those who were poor and who were calling for help.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{They also approved of me because} I aided orphans who had no fathers to help them.
<verse eid="JOB 29:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 29:13"/>People who had been suffering and were about to die praised me {for rescuing them}.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I made widows very happy by helping them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 29:14"/>It was in my character to make sure that I and others did what was right.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, it was in my character to make sure that people treated one another in a way that was just.
<verse eid="JOB 29:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 29:15"/>I helped people who could not see, as if I were seeing for them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I helped people who could not walk, as if I were walking for them.
<verse eid="JOB 29:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 29:16"/>I protected poor people the way a father protects his children.
</para>
  <para style="q2">In the courts, I defended even people who were strangers to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 29:17"/>I made wicked people stop oppressing others.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It was as if I were breaking the jaws of a wild animal
</para>
  <para style="q2">to make it drop its victim from its teeth.
<verse eid="JOB 29:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 29:18"/>At that time I thought, ‘I am certainly going to live a long life,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then I will die at home with my family.
<verse eid="JOB 29:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 29:19"/>I am like a tree whose roots reach down into the water
</para>
  <para style="q2">and whose branches become wet with dew each night.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 29:20"/>People continually honor me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I stay strong like a new bow.’
<verse eid="JOB 29:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 29:21"/>When I spoke, people waited to hear what I would say.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They remained silent until I advised them what they should do.
<verse eid="JOB 29:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 29:22"/>After I finished speaking, they did not say anything,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because what I had said was so satisfying.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 29:23"/>They were as eager to hear me speak as farmers are for it to rain.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They listened to me with great appreciation, just as the ground seems to welcome the rain that falls in the spring.
<verse eid="JOB 29:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 29:24"/>I helped people who felt discouraged.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I remained cheerful even as I helped them deal with difficult situations.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 29:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 29:25"/>As their leader, I decided what things would be good for them to do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They respected me, just as soldiers respect the king who commands their army.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I was kind and sympathetic, like someone who comforts people who are mourning.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 29:25"/><verse eid="JOB 29:25"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 29"/>
  <chapter number="30" style="c" sid="JOB 30"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 30:1"/>But now, men who are younger than I am make fun of me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are men whose fathers I would not even have hired to help my dogs guard my sheep!
<verse eid="JOB 30:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 30:2"/>And I would not hire any of those young men to work for me either.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are already feeble.
<verse eid="JOB 30:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 30:3"/>{No one hires them,} so they are poor and go hungry and have become thin.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They have to eat roots
</para>
  <para style="q2">that they find in places where no crops have grown for a long time.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 30:4"/>They look among bushes to find leaves that they can eat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They pull up broom trees and eat their roots.
<verse eid="JOB 30:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 30:5"/>{When they come near villages,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">people shout at them, as they would at a thief,
</para>
  <para style="q2">to make them leave their area.
<verse eid="JOB 30:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 30:6"/>They have to live in riverbeds,
</para>
  <para style="q2">in holes in the ground,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and in caves on the sides of cliffs.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 30:7"/>{During the day} they huddle under thorn bushes.
</para>
  <para style="q2">There they howl like animals because they are so hungry.

<verse eid="JOB 30:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 30:8"/>They are people without good sense,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they have never achieved anything significant.
</para>
  <para style="q1">People force them to leave their towns.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 30:9"/>But these despicable young men sing songs to make fun of me!
</para>
  <para style="q2">If they think God is punishing someone for being a sinner, they call that person ‘another Job.’
<verse eid="JOB 30:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 30:10"/>They hate me and they stay away from me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but if we happen to meet, they spit right in my face.
<verse eid="JOB 30:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 30:11"/>I am not able to defend myself against these young men, because God has taken away my strength.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He has humiliated me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So they can do whatever they want to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 30:12"/>Gangs of these young men threaten me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They shove me aside when they pass by.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They think of new ways to hurt me.
<verse eid="JOB 30:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 30:13"/>I am not able to escape from them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even as they keep doing worse things to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one else would do the things they do to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 30:14"/>It is as if I were a city and they were an army that had broken through its wall
</para>
  <para style="q2">and sent in waves of troops to destroy the city.
<verse eid="JOB 30:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 30:15"/>I am afraid of many things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have no dignity left,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I do not expect that I will ever get out of this situation.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 30:16"/>I feel as if I am about to die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but in the meantime, I am suffering greatly.
<verse eid="JOB 30:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 30:17"/>My body aches during the night.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I feel pain continually.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 30:18"/>I have such a serious disease that my skin no longer looks the same.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It seems as if I will always be sick.
<verse eid="JOB 30:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 30:19"/>God has made me suffer so much that I have been sitting here on the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am even beginning to look like the dust and ashes in which I have been sitting.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 30:20"/>Even though I pray to you, God, you do not respond.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I try to get your attention, but you do not do anything to help me.
<verse eid="JOB 30:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 30:21"/>You used to be kind to me, but you are not kind to me anymore.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You are using your mighty power to make me suffer.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 30:22"/>It is as if you have sent a great wind to pick me up and carry me away.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if you are making a violent storm blow me around.
<verse eid="JOB 30:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 30:23"/>Certainly I know that you are going to make me die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That is what happens to everyone who ever lives.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 30:24"/>However, when someone falls down, he certainly reaches out with his hand for help.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When someone is in trouble, he certainly calls for help.
<verse eid="JOB 30:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 30:25"/>{So it is appropriate for me to ask for your help now, God.}
</para>
  <para style="q1">After all, I wept for people myself when they were experiencing troubles.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I felt sorry for poor people.
<verse eid="JOB 30:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 30:26"/>I am asking for help because although I expected good things to happen to me, evil things happened instead.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It was as if I was waiting for dawn but another night started instead of a new day.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 30:27"/>I am very distressed all the time.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I suffer during every day.
<verse eid="JOB 30:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 30:28"/>I look as if I have to work outside all day to make a living.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I humble myself by pleading openly for people to help me.
<verse eid="JOB 30:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 30:29"/>{No one wants to come near me.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if I am a jackal or ostrich living out in the wilderness.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 30:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 30:30"/>My skin has become dark, and it is peeling off.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have a fever that causes my body to feel as if it were burning up.
<verse eid="JOB 30:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 30:31"/>Previously, if you came to my house, you would hear musical instruments playing happy songs.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you came now, you would only hear people crying and singing sad songs.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 30:31"/><verse eid="JOB 30:31"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 30"/>
  <chapter number="31" style="c" sid="JOB 31"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 31:1"/>I have made a solemn promise to myself that I will not look at a woman in a lustful way.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So I would not look at a woman who was not my wife and want to have sexual relations with her.
<verse eid="JOB 31:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 31:2"/>{If I did not do what I promised,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">God who is in heaven would certainly punish me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Shaddai in heaven would make sure that I got what I deserved.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 31:3"/>{I would not have done wrong in that way, because I know that} unrighteous people experience calamities.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People who do what is wrong experience disasters {when God punishes them}.
<verse eid="JOB 31:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 31:4"/>I know that God sees everything that I do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He is aware of each individual action that I take.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 31:5"/>I solemnly declare that I have not lied to others
</para>
  <para style="q2">and that I have not tried to deceive others.
<verse eid="JOB 31:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 31:6"/>I only ask God to judge me fairly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If he does that, he will find that I am innocent.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 31:7"/>I solemnly declare that I have continually acted properly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not seen wrong things to do and then done them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am not guilty of committing sin.
<verse eid="JOB 31:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 31:8"/>If I have been lustful or deceptive, I hope that when I plant seeds, someone else will harvest the crops and eat them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Or may someone uproot the crops that are growing in my fields!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 31:9"/>I solemnly declare that I have not been attracted to another man’s wife.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not made secret arrangements with a woman to have sexual relations with her while her husband was away.
<verse eid="JOB 31:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 31:10"/>If I have done that, I hope that my own wife will be attracted to another man
</para>
  <para style="q2">and that she will have sexual relations with him.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 31:11"/>If I had sexual relations with another man’s wife, that would be a terrible sin.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If I were caught and her husband brought me to court, the judges would certainly decide to punish me.
<verse eid="JOB 31:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 31:12"/>{I would have to pay a fine that would cost me everything I owned.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">It would be as if a fire had burned everything I had right to the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It would be as if someone pulled out all the crops that were growing in my fields.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 31:13"/>I solemnly declare that when my male or female servants complained to me about something,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I took their complaint seriously and made sure that I treated them fairly.
<verse eid="JOB 31:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 31:14"/>I knew that if I did not treat my servants fairly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I could not expect God to be merciful to me when he judged me.
</para>
  <para style="q1">If God came to help my servants,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I could offer no excuse for the way I had treated them.
<verse eid="JOB 31:14"/><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 31:15"/>{I am no different from my servants, so I have no right to mistreat them.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">The same God created each one of us.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16-18" style="v" sid="JOB 31:16-18"/>I solemnly declare that from the time that I was young,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have taken care of orphans and I have protected widows.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I have not failed to provide poor people with the things that they needed.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not caused widows to lose hope.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not eaten all of my food by myself and not shared it with orphans.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:16-18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 31:19"/>I solemnly declare that I provided clothes for poor people when I saw that they were suffering from lack of clothing.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have provided warm outer garments for needy people.
<verse eid="JOB 31:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 31:20"/>I gave them warm clothes that my servants had woven from the wool of my sheep.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The poor people thanked me for helping them.
<verse eid="JOB 31:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 31:21"/>I solemnly declare that I never threatened an orphan who brought a case against me in the public square.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even if I knew that the other elders would favor me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I made sure that the orphan received fair treatment.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 31:22"/>If I have neglected poor people or oppressed orphans,
</para>
  <para style="q2">may the arm {that I did not use to help them but used instead to threaten them}
</para>
  <para style="q2">fall right out of its socket!
<verse eid="JOB 31:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 31:23"/>I have not done wrong things such as I have been describing,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because I respect God and I know that he punishes sin.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would not sin against such a majestic God!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 31:24"/>I solemnly declare that I have not depended on wealth
</para>
  <para style="q2">as a source of security and power.
<verse eid="JOB 31:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 31:25"/>I solemnly declare that I did not think that I was better than other people
</para>
  <para style="q2">because I had many possessions and because I had become very rich.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 31:26"/>I solemnly declare that I never looked at the sun when it was shining
</para>
  <para style="q2">or at the beautiful moon
<verse eid="JOB 31:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 31:27"/>and made a gesture to worship them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I never wanted to do that.
<verse eid="JOB 31:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 31:28"/>If I had worshiped the sun or the moon and people found out that I had,
</para>
  <para style="q2">the judges would certainly decide to punish me for that too,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because I would have been worshiping a false god instead of the true God.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29-30" style="v" sid="JOB 31:29-30"/>I solemnly declare that I have not been glad when my enemies have suffered misfortunes.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have not thought that I was better than they were when they experienced disasters.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I have not sinned by praying that God would curse people who hated me
</para>
  <para style="q2">and cause them to die.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:29-30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31-32" style="v" sid="JOB 31:31-32"/>I solemnly declare that I have invited travelers to stay in my house.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Visitors have not had to sleep in the streets.
</para>
  <para style="q2">My servants talk about how I give food to anyone who needs it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:31-32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 31:33"/>I know that people generally try to hide their sins.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But I solemnly declare that I have not done that.
<verse eid="JOB 31:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 31:34"/>I did not let fear of what people would say about me or think of me
</para>
  <para style="q2">keep me from admitting that I had done wrong and making amends.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:34"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="JOB 31:35"/>I wish that I had someone who would judge my case against God!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I hereby declare that all that I have just said is true.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Now I wish that Shaddai would state his case.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would like to see his written statement of charges against me.
<verse eid="JOB 31:35"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="JOB 31:36"/>If Shaddai wrote out his charges against me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would not be ashamed, because I know that I am innocent.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I would display the charges publicly{, because they would actually list what I had not done}.
<verse eid="JOB 31:36"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="JOB 31:37"/>I would explain to God in detail
</para>
  <para style="q2">how I was innocent of each charge.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would approach him as a ruler would: without being afraid.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 31:37"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="JOB 31:38"/>I solemnly declare that I have not defrauded the people who farm my land.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They have no reason to cry out for justice against me.

<verse eid="JOB 31:38"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="JOB 31:39"/>I solemnly declare that I have taken only a fair share of the crops that people have grown on my land.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I have made sure that the people who grew crops on my land kept enough of them to feed their families.
<verse eid="JOB 31:39"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="JOB 31:40"/>If I have cheated the people who farm my land,
</para>
  <para style="q2">then I wish that thorns would grow in my fields instead of wheat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I wish that bad weeds would grow there instead of barley!”
</para>
  <para style="q1">After this, Job said nothing further to defend himself to his three friends.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 31:40"/><verse eid="JOB 31:40"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 31"/>
  <chapter number="32" style="c" sid="JOB 32"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 32:1"/>Then Job’s three friends stopped replying to him, because Job was confident that he was innocent.
<verse eid="JOB 32:1"/><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 32:2"/>Then a man named Elihu{, who had been listening as Job spoke with his friends,} became very angry with Job. Elihu was the son of Barakel. He was a Buzite and he belonged to the clan of Ram. He became angry because Job continued to claim that he was innocent and that God had been wrong to punish him.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:2"/><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 32:3"/>Elihu also became angry with Job’s three friends. He became angry because they had insisted that Job must have done many things that were wrong, but they had not been able to prove any of their accusations.
<verse eid="JOB 32:3"/><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 32:4"/>Now Elihu was much younger than Job’s three friends. That was why he had let them speak to Job first.
<verse eid="JOB 32:4"/><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 32:5"/>But when Elihu realized that the three men had nothing more to say to Job, he became very angry.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:5"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 32:6"/>This is what Elihu said. (He was the son of Barakel. He was a Buzite.)
</para>
  <para style="q1">“I am young, and you all are much older than I am.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So I was hesitant. I was afraid to tell you what I was thinking.
<verse eid="JOB 32:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 32:7"/>I said to myself, ‘I should let the people who are older speak.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Older people should be able to explain how to understand things wisely.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 32:8"/>However, even as I was saying that, I felt the Spirit of God stirring within me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Yes, I felt Shaddai inspiring me so that I could understand.
<verse eid="JOB 32:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 32:9"/>Humble people can also be wise.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Younger people can also recognize the right thing to do.
<verse eid="JOB 32:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 32:10"/>So now I want to ask you, ‘Please listen to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Let me too say what I have been thinking.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 32:11"/>Now I let the three of you speak first.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I waited while you thought carefully about what you should say,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I listened to your arguments.
<verse eid="JOB 32:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 32:12"/>I paid careful attention to what you were saying.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But in the end, none of you was able to prove that what Job said was wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">None of you could respond to his arguments.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 32:13"/>So do not tell yourselves, ‘We have discovered what is wise!’
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is God who must show Job that he is wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Someone relying on human thinking will not be able to do that.
<verse eid="JOB 32:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 32:14"/>You were angry with Job, and so you have not answered him well.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But Job has said nothing to make me angry, so I will not answer him the way you have.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 32:15"/>The three of you are just sitting there in a daze, not saying anything more,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because you cannot think of what to say to Job.
<verse eid="JOB 32:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 32:16"/>And since you are not speaking, since you have stopped answering Job,
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not feel that I need to wait any longer before speaking to Job myself.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 32:17"/>So now I too will respond to Job.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will share my view of his situation.
<verse eid="JOB 32:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 32:18"/>I must speak, because I have plenty to say,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and something inside me is forcing me to say it.
<verse eid="JOB 32:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 32:19"/>I feel as if I am a container of wine that is stretching more and more
</para>
  <para style="q2">and that will soon burst because of the fermentation.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 32:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 32:20"/>I must speak, and then I will be able to rest from the effort of restraining myself from speaking.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So I will now say something in reply to all of you.
<verse eid="JOB 32:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 32:21"/>I will not favor either Job or you his friends.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will not try to flatter anyone.
<verse eid="JOB 32:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 32:22"/>Indeed, I have not gotten into the habit of flattering people,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because I believe that if I did that, God would quickly destroy me.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 32:22"/><verse eid="JOB 32:22"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 32"/>
  <chapter number="33" style="c" sid="JOB 33"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 33:1"/>So now, Job, I ask you to listen carefully
</para>
  <para style="q2">to everything that I am going to say.
<verse eid="JOB 33:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 33:2"/>I am ready to speak to you and tell you what I think.
<verse eid="JOB 33:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 33:3"/>What I say will express my sincere feelings of concern for you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will only tell you what is genuinely true.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 33:4"/>You can trust me as someone whom God’s Spirit created.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You can trust me as someone whom Shaddai made alive.
<verse eid="JOB 33:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 33:5"/>{Once I have spoken,} respond to me if you can.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Think carefully about how you will reply to me.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 33:6"/>Now you wanted God to answer you, and I will do that for him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But I am human, just like you,
<verse eid="JOB 33:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 33:7"/>so you do not need to be afraid of me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will not speak to you harshly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 33:8"/>I listened carefully to you as you spoke,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and this is what I heard you say:
<verse eid="JOB 33:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 33:9"/>‘I am righteous. I have not committed any sins.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am innocent. I have not done things that are wrong.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 33:10"/>Nevertheless, God looks for excuses to punish me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He treats me as if I were his enemy.
<verse eid="JOB 33:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 33:11"/>He judges me by very strict standards.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He closely observes everything that I do.’
<verse eid="JOB 33:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 33:12"/>However, what you have said is incorrect.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will explain why.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{You do not realize what God is actually doing, since} it is beyond human understanding.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 33:13"/>You should not be protesting that God does not respond to what anyone says.
<verse eid="JOB 33:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 33:14"/>God actually does speak to people in various ways.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People just do not realize how he is doing that.
<verse eid="JOB 33:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 33:15"/>Sometimes God speaks to people at night in dreams and visions
</para>
  <para style="q2">when they are sound asleep in bed.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 33:16"/>God reveals things to people at those times.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He shows them that they need to change the way that they have been living.
<verse eid="JOB 33:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 33:17"/>God speaks in that way to get people to stop doing evil deeds.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God wants to prevent people from sinning because they have become proud.
<verse eid="JOB 33:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 33:18"/>In this way, God prevents people from dying.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Otherwise, someone might kill them violently.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 33:19"/>God also corrects people by making them so ill that they have to lie in bed.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They feel constant agony in their bodies.
<verse eid="JOB 33:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 33:20"/>They feel so sick that they do not want to eat anything,
</para>
  <para style="q2">not even their favorite foods.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 33:21"/>They lose so much weight that there appears to be no flesh between their skin and their bones.
<verse eid="JOB 33:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 33:22"/>They become so sick that they are about to die.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The angels who cause people to die start coming to get them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 33:23"/>However, sometimes a special kind of angel may come to a person
</para>
  <para style="q2">and tell him on behalf of God how he can change the way that he has been living so that he will not die.
<verse eid="JOB 33:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 33:24"/>{If the person changes the way that he has been living,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">then God will forgive him.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God will tell the angels who cause people to die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">‘Do not cause this person to die!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I can see that I should allow him to live, because now he will do what is right.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 33:25"/>When that happens, the person will become healthy again and his skin will appear as fresh as the skin of a child.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He will be as strong again as he was when he was young.
<verse eid="JOB 33:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 33:26"/>Then God will once again treat him as a righteous person.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He will pray to God, and God will answer his prayer.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He will come into God’s presence joyfully.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 33:27"/>He will sing a song of thanksgiving and tell the people who are listening,
</para>
  <para style="q1">‘I sinned; yes, I did things that were not right,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but God did not punish me in the way that I deserved.
<verse eid="JOB 33:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 33:28"/>He has saved me from going to the place where dead people are.
</para>
  <para style="q2">And so I will continue to enjoy being alive!’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 33:29"/>Indeed, God does this for many people.
<verse eid="JOB 33:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 33:30"/>He keeps them from going to the place where dead people are.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That way they can continue to enjoy being alive.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 33:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 33:31"/>So Job, please listen to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do not say anything right now; just allow me to speak.
<verse eid="JOB 33:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 33:32"/>{After I have spoken, then} if you have something more that you want to say to me,
</para>
  <para style="q2">say it, because I would like to show that you are innocent.
<verse eid="JOB 33:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 33:33"/>However, if you have nothing more that you want to say, then just listen to me.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will explain to you how you can understand your situation in a wise way.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 33:33"/><verse eid="JOB 33:33"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 33"/>
  <chapter number="34" style="c" sid="JOB 34"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 34:1"/>Then Elihu continued to speak, and he said,
<verse eid="JOB 34:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 34:2"/>“I want everyone who is wise to hear what I am about to say.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I want everyone who knows a lot to listen to me.
<verse eid="JOB 34:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 34:3"/>I want you to listen carefully to me because when people hear others speak,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they need to decide whether what the others are saying is right or wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They do this just as people taste food to decide whether it is good or bad.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 34:4"/>I would like all of us to decide together who is right, Job or his friends.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I would like us to find out together what is a good way to understand his situation.
<verse eid="JOB 34:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 34:5"/>We need to consider this carefully together because Job has said, ‘I am innocent,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but God has not judged me fairly.’
<verse eid="JOB 34:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 34:6"/>Job has said he would not lie about whether he has done what is right.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He has spoken as if God had shot him with an arrow and wounded him so badly that he will die,
</para>
  <para style="q2">even though he is innocent.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 34:7"/>I have not heard anyone else speak the way Job does.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People drink water to satisfy their thirst, and mocking God seems to satisfy Job.
<verse eid="JOB 34:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 34:8"/>His friends are people who do what is wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He spends time with wicked people.
<verse eid="JOB 34:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 34:9"/>We know that this is true about him because he has said, ‘It is useless for people to want to please God.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 34:10"/>So, all of you who understand things well, listen to me!
</para>
  <para style="q1">God would never consider doing anything that was wicked!
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, Shaddai would never consider doing anything that was wrong!
<verse eid="JOB 34:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 34:11"/>We can be confident that God treats people fairly because we can see that he rewards or punishes people properly for what they have done.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God gives people what they deserve for the manner in which they have lived.
<verse eid="JOB 34:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 34:12"/>No, God never does anything wicked.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Shaddai always treats people fairly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 34:13"/>God did not need anyone to give him the authority to rule the people who live on the earth.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He did not need anyone to put him in control of all the people in the world.
<verse eid="JOB 34:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 34:14"/>If God ever thought only about himself and not also about the world he created, if God ever stopped keeping people and animals alive,
<verse eid="JOB 34:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 34:15"/>then every living thing would die immediately,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and the bodies of people would soon become soil again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 34:16"/>So, Job, if you truly want to understand,
</para>
  <para style="q2">then listen carefully to what I am about to say.
<verse eid="JOB 34:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 34:17"/>God could certainly not hate what is right and still rule the world.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So you really cannot say that God, who is righteous and powerful, has done something wrong.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 34:18"/>God tells some kings that they are worthless,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he says to some officials that they are wicked.
<verse eid="JOB 34:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 34:19"/>God does not treat rulers better than he treats other people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does not treat rich people more respectfully than he treats poor people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">After all, God created all people.
<verse eid="JOB 34:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 34:20"/>If people use their power to oppress others,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God punishes them by killing them.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Those people are not expecting to have troubles and die, and humans do not kill them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but God kills them very quickly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 34:21"/>God punishes oppressive people because he sees everything that people do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God is aware of each individual action that a person does.
<verse eid="JOB 34:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 34:22"/>Wicked people can not escape from God
</para>
  <para style="q2">by hiding in places where it is very dark.
<verse eid="JOB 34:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 34:23"/>Now God does not need to examine a person further
</para>
  <para style="q2">before passing judgment on that person.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 34:24"/>God does not need to investigate what people have done.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He knows whether important people have done wrong, and he destroys them if they have.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He then appoints others to take their places.
<verse eid="JOB 34:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 34:25"/>Because he already knows what they have done,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he removes them quickly and gets rid of them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 34:26"/>God kills them because of the wicked things that they have done.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Many people see him do it.
<verse eid="JOB 34:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 34:27"/>God kills them because they stopped doing what he wanted them to do
</para>
  <para style="q2">and did not pay attention to his commands.
<verse eid="JOB 34:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 34:28"/>They mistreated poor people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those poor people prayed to God for help,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and God answered their prayers.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 34:29"/>Yet even if God decides not to punish wicked people right away,
</para>
  <para style="q2">no one can criticize him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If God does not reveal what he is doing,
</para>
  <para style="q2">no one can understand it.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God controls every nation and every person.
<verse eid="JOB 34:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 34:30"/>God makes sure that those who rule honor him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He makes sure that rulers do not oppress the people whom they rule.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 34:31"/>Suppose someone says to God,
</para>
  <para style="q2">‘I recognize that I am suffering because I have sinned.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I do not want to sin any longer.
<verse eid="JOB 34:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 34:32"/>Please show me what sins I have committed.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will stop doing anything that is evil.’
<verse eid="JOB 34:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 34:33"/>If a person said that to God,
</para>
  <para style="q2">do you think that God would continue to punish him?
</para>
  <para style="q1">Now you have been saying that God does not treat people fairly,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so it is important for you to decide what you think God would do.
</para>
  <para style="q1">I will not suggest the answer myself.
</para>
  <para style="q2">We would like to hear you say what you think about this.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 34:34"/>{But unfortunately, I believe that} when people who have good sense
</para>
  <para style="q2">and people who are wise hear my question and your answer,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they will say to me,
<verse eid="JOB 34:34"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="JOB 34:35"/>‘Job is speaking ignorantly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">What he says does not make sense.’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 34:35"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="JOB 34:36"/>It would be good if God put Job on trial
</para>
  <para style="q2">and investigated every last detail of his case.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God should do that because Job has been speaking just as wicked people speak.
<verse eid="JOB 34:36"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="JOB 34:37"/>God should put Job on trial because in addition to sinning,
</para>
  <para style="q2">he is saying that God does not treat people fairly.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Knowing that we were watching, he clapped his hands to insult God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He has made long speeches to say that God has punished him unjustly.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 34:37"/><verse eid="JOB 34:37"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 34"/>
  <chapter number="35" style="c" sid="JOB 35"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 35:1"/>Then Elihu continued to speak, and he said,
<verse eid="JOB 35:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 35:2"/>“Job, it is not right for you to say that you have been doing what is right
</para>
  <para style="q2">but God has been doing what is wrong.
<verse eid="JOB 35:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 35:3"/>You are basically saying that when you ask,
</para>
  <para style="q2">‘What good things have I received because I have not sinned?
</para>
  <para style="q2">How am I better off than I would be if I had sinned?’

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 35:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 35:4"/>I will answer those questions for you,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I will also respond to what your three friends have said.
<verse eid="JOB 35:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 35:5"/>Job, look up at the sky.
</para>
  <para style="q2">See how high the clouds are above you!
</para>
  <para style="q2">{God is far higher than that!}

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 35:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 35:6"/>So if you sin, that does not harm God at all.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even if you do many wrong things, that does not hurt him.
<verse eid="JOB 35:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 35:7"/>On the other hand, if you do what is right, that does not make God better off.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No, you are not giving God something that he does not already have.
<verse eid="JOB 35:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 35:8"/>The only ones who suffer if you are wicked are other people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The only ones you might help by being good are other humans.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 35:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 35:9"/>People call for others to help them because of the many things that wicked people do that make them suffer.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They call for help because of the things that powerful people do to oppress them.
<verse eid="JOB 35:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 35:10"/>But people do not ask God, their Creator, to help them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People do not expect God to enable them to sing joyful songs because he has rescued them.
<verse eid="JOB 35:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 35:11"/>People do not realize that God wants to teach them how to live wisely.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does that for people, not for wild animals or for the birds in the sky.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 35:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 35:12"/>Even when people do pray to God for help,
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does not answer them,
</para>
  <para style="q2">because they arrogantly continue to do what is wrong.
<verse eid="JOB 35:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 35:13"/>God, the Almighty One, does not respond at all
</para>
  <para style="q2">when people pray but do not really mean what they are saying.
<verse eid="JOB 35:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 35:14"/>You are telling God that you have made your case against him
</para>
  <para style="q2">but that you are still waiting for him to answer your accusations.
</para>
  <para style="q1">You are saying that God has not been helping you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Since you say those things, you should certainly not expect God to respond to you!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 35:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 35:15"/>You also say that God does not pay much attention when people commit sins,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and so he does not become angry and punish them.
<verse eid="JOB 35:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 35:16"/>My friends, you see that Job has said things that are completely useless.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He says many things without knowing what he is talking about.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 35:16"/><verse eid="JOB 35:16"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 35"/>
  <chapter number="36" style="c" sid="JOB 36"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 36:1"/>Then Elihu said some more things to Job:
<verse eid="JOB 36:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 36:2"/>“Job, be patient with me a little longer as I explain things to you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Be patient, because I have more to say in order to show that God does not do anything wrong.
<verse eid="JOB 36:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 36:3"/>I will tell you what I have learned from many sources.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will show that God, who made me, always does what is right.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 36:4"/>You can indeed listen patiently because I will not say anything to you that is false.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am here with you as someone who understands things well.
<verse eid="JOB 36:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 36:5"/>Truly, God is very powerful, but he values every person.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He understands everything completely.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 36:6"/>He does not allow wicked people to remain alive,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but he helps people whom others have been oppressing.
<verse eid="JOB 36:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 36:7"/>He always protects people who are righteous.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He makes them prosper as if they were kings,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he causes others to honor them continually.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 36:8"/>Sometimes people suffer great troubles because they have done wrong things.
<verse eid="JOB 36:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 36:9"/>When they do, God shows them what they have done wrong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He shows them that they committed these sins because they were proud.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 36:10"/>He enables them to understand what he is teaching them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He commands them to stop sinning.
<verse eid="JOB 36:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 36:11"/>If they obey him and once again live as he wishes,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they will prosper and be happy for as long as they live.
<verse eid="JOB 36:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 36:12"/>But if they do not obey him,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they will die violently
</para>
  <para style="q2">because they did not seek to understand how God wanted them to live.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 36:13"/>People who do not respect God continue to be angry with God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They do not ask God to help them
</para>
  <para style="q2">even when they are suffering because of their sins.
<verse eid="JOB 36:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 36:14"/>They can only make a living in a dishonorable way,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they die while they are still young.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 36:15"/>God actually uses suffering to keep people from perishing because of their sins.
</para>
  <para style="q2">By making them suffer, he causes them to listen to what he is telling them.
<verse eid="JOB 36:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 36:16"/>Job, I believe that God wants to bring you out of your troubles
</para>
  <para style="q2">and allow you to live without distress.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He wants you to be happy and prosperous.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 36:17"/>However, you are still saying bad things about God the way a wicked person would.
</para>
  <para style="q2">That is why God is punishing you so severely.
<verse eid="JOB 36:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 36:18"/>You are angry with God, but do not let that lead you to say disrespectful things about God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you did that, God would not accept even a very large bribe to pardon you.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 36:19"/>God would not want your money and so pardon you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He would not take gold or a great deal of money as a bribe.
<verse eid="JOB 36:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 36:20"/>You should not wish that you could die so that your suffering would be over.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Once people die, they are gone.
<verse eid="JOB 36:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 36:21"/>Be careful not to commit sin.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It seems that you would rather defy God than learn what he wants to teach you through suffering.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 36:22"/>Truly, God is so powerful that he can do great things.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He is better than any other teacher.
<verse eid="JOB 36:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 36:23"/>No one has ever told him what he should do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one has ever had to tell him that what he was doing was wrong.
<verse eid="JOB 36:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 36:24"/>People have often sung songs to praise him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You too should remember what he has done and praise him for it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 36:25"/>Everyone has seen what God has done,
</para>
  <para style="q2">but even so, people only understand it a little.
<verse eid="JOB 36:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 36:26"/>How great God is! We are not able to understand how great he is,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and we are not able to determine how old he is.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 36:27"/>Indeed, God draws water up from the earth and puts it in clouds
</para>
  <para style="q2">and causes it to become rain.
<verse eid="JOB 36:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 36:28"/>This rain pours down from the clouds
</para>
  <para style="q2">and abundant showers fall on the earth, where people live.
<verse eid="JOB 36:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 36:29"/>No one can really understand how the clouds spread across the sky
</para>
  <para style="q2">or how it thunders in the sky where God lives.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 36:30"/>See how God causes lightning to flash all around him!
</para>
  <para style="q2">The lightning is so bright that it lights up even the deep ocean.
<verse eid="JOB 36:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 36:31"/>Because his storms are so great, God uses them to punish wicked people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But he also uses the rain from these storms to provide abundant food for righteous people.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 36:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 36:32"/>It is as if God picks up lightning in his hands
</para>
  <para style="q2">and sends it to strike where he wants it to.
<verse eid="JOB 36:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 36:33"/>God makes thunder to announce that there will be a storm.
</para>
  <para style="q2">We also know that a storm is coming when we hear cattle making extra noise.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 36:33"/><verse eid="JOB 36:33"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 36"/>
  <chapter number="37" style="c" sid="JOB 37"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 37:1"/>My heart really pounds when I think about that!
</para>
  <para style="q2">I feel as if it is jumping around inside me.
<verse eid="JOB 37:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 37:2"/>Listen, all of you, to the thunder!
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is as if God is roaring!
<verse eid="JOB 37:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 37:3"/>God makes such loud thunder that people can hear it throughout the land.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God makes such bright lighting that people can see it from far away.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 37:4"/>After lightning flashes, we hear thunder,
</para>
  <para style="q2">which sounds like God speaking very loudly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then, after the thunder, God makes more lightning.
<verse eid="JOB 37:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 37:5"/>When we hear thunder, it is as if God is shouting in an amazing way.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God does things that are too wonderful for us to understand.
<verse eid="JOB 37:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 37:6"/>For example, God can cause snow to cover the ground,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and when he tells a rain shower to rain even harder, it becomes a downpour.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 37:7"/>When God makes it rain so hard, everyone has to stop working outside.
</para>
  <para style="q2">This shows all people, whom he has made, what great things he is able to do.
<verse eid="JOB 37:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 37:8"/>When it rains, the animals go into their hiding places
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they stay there until the rain stops.
<verse eid="JOB 37:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 37:9"/>Storms come from the place where they start,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and cold winds come from the north.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 37:10"/>In the winter, cold winds turn water into ice.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The water in lakes freezes solid.
<verse eid="JOB 37:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 37:11"/>God truly fills the clouds with water droplets.
</para>
  <para style="q2">He makes lightning flash from many clouds.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 37:12"/>He causes the clouds to move around over the places where people live
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that they can accomplish everything that he wants them to do.
<verse eid="JOB 37:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 37:13"/>God may send storms to punish people.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God may send storms to water the land that he made.
</para>
  <para style="q2">God may send storms to help people by providing rain to make their crops grow.
</para>
  <para style="q1">God makes all of these things happen.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 37:14"/>Job, listen to what I am saying.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Just think about the wonderful things that God does.
<verse eid="JOB 37:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 37:15"/>Are you able to explain how God arranges the clouds in the sky and makes lightning flash down from them?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 37:16"/>Are you able to explain how God is able to make clouds float in the sky?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Can you understand all of the wonderful things that God does?
</para>
  <para style="q2">God understands everything completely!
<verse eid="JOB 37:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 37:17"/>Are you able to explain how God can bring in stifling air from the desert
</para>
  <para style="q2">and make it so hot that you cannot stay cool if you wear clothes?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 37:18"/>Are you able to explain how God made the sky
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that it would stay firmly in place above the earth?
<verse eid="JOB 37:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 37:19"/>I doubt that you could tell us what to say to God.
</para>
  <para style="q2">People do not know enough to be able to defend themselves if they spoke with God.
<verse eid="JOB 37:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 37:20"/>I would not dare tell God that I needed to speak with him!
</para>
  <para style="q2">If I did that, then he might destroy me!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 37:21"/>You know that people can not look directly at the sun
</para>
  <para style="q2">when it shines brightly in the sky after the wind has blown the clouds away.
<verse eid="JOB 37:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 37:22"/>When God appears, he has glittering light all around him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">His glory is so bright that it makes us afraid.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{So if we cannot even look at the sun, how could we look at God?}

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 37:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 37:23"/>Shaddai has very great power,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and we do not know how to get near to him.
</para>
  <para style="q1">He always acts righteously,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and he never mistreats anyone.
<verse eid="JOB 37:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 37:24"/>That is why everyone should have great respect for him.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Any people who think that they are wise do not impress him.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 37:24"/><verse eid="JOB 37:24"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 37"/>
  <chapter number="38" style="c" sid="JOB 38"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 38:1"/>Then Yahweh spoke to Job from inside the storm that had been approaching. He said to him,
<verse eid="JOB 38:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 38:2"/>“You have been speaking ignorantly, and as a result,
</para>
  <para style="q2">you have been making it harder for people to understand my actions.
<verse eid="JOB 38:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 38:3"/>I am going to ask you some questions,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so get ready to answer them directly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 38:4"/>Where were you when I began to create the earth?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Since you claim to know so much, tell me how I did that.
<verse eid="JOB 38:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 38:5"/>Who decided how large the earth would be?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Who made sure that it was the right size?
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you know as much as you claim, you ought to be able to tell me that.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6-7" style="v" sid="JOB 38:6-7"/>What keeps the earth from collapsing?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Who made sure that everything on the earth would be in the right position?
</para>
  <para style="q1">The earth was so beautiful when I made it that it was as if the first stars all sang a song to celebrate it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">All of the angels gave a great cheer when they saw the earth.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:6-7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 38:8"/>When I made the land, the sea could have flowed over it.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Who prevented the sea from flooding the earth?
<verse eid="JOB 38:8"/><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 38:9"/>Now the clouds in the sky float over the sea as if they were its clothing.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Night and day happen at sea just as they do on land.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 38:10"/>I decided how much of the area under the sky the sea would cover.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I made barriers at the shore of the sea to keep it off the land.
<verse eid="JOB 38:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 38:11"/>I proclaimed that the sea could only come up so far on the shore and not come any farther.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I said that its great waves could only flow up to a certain place on the land.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 38:12"/>Job, have you ever commanded a day to begin?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Have you told the sun where it needed to rise on a certain day?
<verse eid="JOB 38:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 38:13"/>When day comes, wicked people all around the world
</para>
  <para style="q2">stop doing the bad things they do at night when no one can see them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 38:14"/>In the light of day, people can see the hills and valleys on earth clearly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The earth seems to take shape the way clay takes shape when people press a seal onto it
</para>
  <para style="q2">or the way folds form in clothing.
<verse eid="JOB 38:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 38:15"/>When daylight comes, wicked people do not have the darkness that they like.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are no longer able to hurt other people.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 38:16"/>Job, have you traveled to the springs at the bottom of the sea?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Have you gone and seen what is at the very bottom of the oceans?
<verse eid="JOB 38:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 38:17"/>Has someone shown you how to get to the place where dead people are?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know where the entrance to that dark place is?
<verse eid="JOB 38:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 38:18"/>Do you know how big the earth is?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Tell me, if you know all these things!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 38:19"/>Do you know how to get to the place where light comes from?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know where darkness comes from?
<verse eid="JOB 38:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 38:20"/>Would you know how to get to those places
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that you could bring light and darkness to them?
<verse eid="JOB 38:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 38:21"/>If you had been alive when I created the world, then you would know the answers to these questions.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But you are not that old, so you certainly do not know the answers.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 38:22"/>Have you gone into the place where I keep snow,
</para>
  <para style="q2">or have you visited the place where I keep hail?
<verse eid="JOB 38:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 38:23"/>I keep snow and hail so that I can use them to defeat one of the armies
</para>
  <para style="q2">when people are fighting wars.
<verse eid="JOB 38:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 38:24"/>Would you know how to get to the place where the sun should rise each morning?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Would you know where the east wind should begin to blow over the earth?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 38:25"/>Could you make rain fall in a certain place?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Could you make a rainstorm go in a certain direction
<verse eid="JOB 38:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 38:26"/>so that it would rain out in the desert,
</para>
  <para style="q2">in a place where no one lives?
<verse eid="JOB 38:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 38:27"/>Would you be able to send rain there to water those barren areas where nothing has grown,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that grass would begin to grow again?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 38:28"/>Do you even know where rain comes from?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know how dew forms on the ground overnight?
<verse eid="JOB 38:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 38:29"/>Do you know how water becomes ice in the winter?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know why dew turns into the frost that covers the ground in the winter?
<verse eid="JOB 38:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 38:30"/>Do you know why the surface of lakes freezes in the winter?
</para>
  <para style="q2">You cannot see the water below the surface; it is as if there is a layer of stone on top of it.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 38:31"/>Can you make stars cluster together, as they do in the cluster you call the Pleiades?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Can you make stars move apart so that they no longer make constellations such as the one you call Orion?
<verse eid="JOB 38:31"/><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 38:32"/>Can you make the constellations of stars appear in the sky at the right time of the year?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Can you make the Big Dipper with its handle appear in the right place in the sky?
<verse eid="JOB 38:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 38:33"/>Do you know how the sun, moon, and stars should move through the sky?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know how they should provide heat and light on the earth?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 38:34"/>Could you shout an order up to a cloud
</para>
  <para style="q2">to make it pour rain down on you?
<verse eid="JOB 38:34"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="JOB 38:35"/>Can you tell flashes of lightning to strike wherever you want?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Would the lightning do what you commanded?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:35"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="JOB 38:36"/>Can you determine where it should be cloudy and where it should be sunny?
<verse eid="JOB 38:36"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="JOB 38:37"/>Are you able to count how many clouds there are?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Can you make it rain
<verse eid="JOB 38:37"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="JOB 38:38"/>after it has not rained for such a long time
</para>
  <para style="q2">that the ground is hard and dry?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:38"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="39-40" style="v" sid="JOB 38:39-40"/>Suppose a lioness and her cubs were crouching in their den or hiding in some bushes, waiting for some animal to pass by that they could kill.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Could you make an animal go by that the lioness could kill
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that she and her cubs could eat the meat and not be hungry anymore?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 38:39-40"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="JOB 38:41"/>Suppose some baby ravens in a nest were chirping loudly because they were hungry.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Suppose they had not eaten in so long that they had become weak.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Could you provide a dead animal whose meat the raven could bring home to its baby birds?

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 38:41"/><verse eid="JOB 38:41"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 38"/>
  <chapter number="39" style="c" sid="JOB 39"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 39:1"/>Job, can you tell when female mountain goats are about to give birth?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Have you watched the wild deer while their calves were being born?
<verse eid="JOB 39:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 39:2"/>Do you know for how long these female animals will be pregnant? Do you know when they will have their babies?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 39:3"/>They crouch down to give birth,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and their babies come out.
<verse eid="JOB 39:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 39:4"/>The young animals grow up in the open fields.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Once they are strong enough to leave their mothers,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they do not return to them again.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 39:5"/>Do you know why some donkeys wander around wild?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know why they are not still working for people?
<verse eid="JOB 39:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 39:6"/>I have given them a home in the desert plain.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are able to live in places where much grass does not grow.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 39:7"/>These wild donkeys do not like the noise in the cities.
</para>
  <para style="q2">In the desert, they do not have to listen to the shouts of those who used to force them to work.
<verse eid="JOB 39:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 39:8"/>They wander over the hills to find food.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They look for any plant that they can eat.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 39:9"/>There are also oxen that are wild.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You could not get one of those to work for you!
</para>
  <para style="q2">It would not stay penned up at night by the place where you put food for your animals!
<verse eid="JOB 39:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 39:10"/>You could not make a wild ox pull a plow
</para>
  <para style="q2">so that it would dig furrows in your fields.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 39:11"/>Even though a wild ox is very strong,
</para>
  <para style="q2">you could not depend on it to do difficult farm work.
<verse eid="JOB 39:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 39:12"/>A wild ox would not help you
</para>
  <para style="q2">to grow more crops than you could grow by yourself.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 39:13"/>Now think about ostriches.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They run very fast, flapping their wings as they go.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But they do not take good care of their offspring.
<verse eid="JOB 39:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 39:14"/>Female ostriches are bad mothers, because lay their eggs and leave them on the ground.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The eggs do stay warm in the sand.
<verse eid="JOB 39:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 39:15"/>But this is still not a good thing to do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Some wild animal could step on the eggs and crush them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 39:16"/>Ostriches do not care well for their chicks.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They act as if the chicks do not belong to them.
</para>
  <para style="q1">They do not seem to be concerned that their chicks might die
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they would have laid their eggs for nothing.
<verse eid="JOB 39:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 39:17"/>Ostriches act this way because I did not give them good instincts.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I did not enable them to understand how they should care for their eggs and chicks.
<verse eid="JOB 39:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 39:18"/>However, ostriches are still awesome birds.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When they run, they can easily run faster than horses!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 39:19"/>But I made horses to be very strong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I put beautiful flowing manes on their necks.
<verse eid="JOB 39:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 39:20"/>I enabled them to leap through the air as if they were locusts.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They snort so loudly that they cause people to be afraid.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 39:21"/>As horses prepare to rush into battle,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they paw the ground, showing off their great strength.

<verse eid="JOB 39:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 39:22"/>Horses seem to disregard danger as they go bravely into battle.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They do not run away when enemy soldiers attack them.
<verse eid="JOB 39:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 39:23"/>As horses run into battle,
</para>
  <para style="q2">the quivers containing their riders’ arrows rattle against their sides.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The spears and javelins that they are carrying flash in the sunlight.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 39:24"/>Horses rush into battle as soon as an officer blows a horn to signal that the army should advance.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The horses run very quickly, and they speedily reach the enemy lines.

<verse eid="JOB 39:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 39:25"/>When they hear someone blow the battle horn, horses neigh excitedly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They can smell a battle even from a distance.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They hear commanders shouting orders to their soldiers {and they know the battle will begin soon}.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 39:26"/>Now think about birds that hunt small animals and other birds.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know how hawks are able to stay in the air as if they were floating?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you know how they sense when it is time to fly to a warmer place for the winter?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 39:27"/>I gave eagles the ability to fly high up into the cliffs and build their nests there.

<verse eid="JOB 39:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 39:28"/>Eagles live on cliffs.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They are safe among the high, pointed rocks there because no animals can get to them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 39:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 39:29"/>From that great height, eagles look for animals that they can kill and eat.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They can see animals that are far away.
<verse eid="JOB 39:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 39:30"/>Eagles kill small animals and bring them back to their nests to feed their chicks.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Eagles also go and eat dead bodies that they see lying on the ground.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 39:30"/><verse eid="JOB 39:30"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 39"/>
  <chapter number="40" style="c" sid="JOB 40"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 40:1"/>Yahweh continued to speak to Job. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 40:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 40:2"/>“Do you still think I am wrong and want to keep arguing with me?
</para>
  <para style="q2">If you want to keep criticizing me, then answer the questions I have asked you!”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:2"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 40:3"/>But Job replied to Yahweh,
<verse eid="JOB 40:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 40:4"/>“Now I realize how insignificant I am. I am not able to answer your questions.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will not say anything in response to them.
<verse eid="JOB 40:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 40:5"/>I have already said more than I should have said.
</para>
  <para style="q2">So now I will say nothing more.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:5"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 40:6"/>Then Yahweh again spoke to Job from inside the storm. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 40:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 40:7"/>“I am going to ask you some more questions, so get ready to answer them directly.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 40:8"/>Do you still want to govern the universe instead of me?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Do you still want to insist that I am doing things wrong and that you would do them right?
<verse eid="JOB 40:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 40:9"/>Do you have the same amount of power as I do?
</para>
  <para style="q2">Does your voice sound as loud as thunder, as mine does?

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 40:10"/>If you want to govern the universe, you must show that you are dignified and noble.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must show that your character is glorious and beautiful.

<verse eid="JOB 40:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 40:11"/>You must express holy wrath against the people who deserve it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">You must recognize which people are being sinfully arrogant and take them out of important positions.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 40:12"/>Yes, you must not allow arrogant people to remain in important positions.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Destroy wicked people quickly!
<verse eid="JOB 40:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 40:13"/>Punish all of them by killing them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Then send them to the place where dead people go.
<verse eid="JOB 40:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 40:14"/>If you did that, I would congratulate you
</para>
  <para style="q2">and admit that you could save yourself by your own ability.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 40:15"/>{But to show you how insignificant you really are,}
</para>
  <para style="q2">I want you to think about a great animal that I created, Behemoth.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I made you, and I also made it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Even though this great beast has the size and strength to hunt and kill other animals, it eats plants as oxen do.
<verse eid="JOB 40:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 40:16"/>Think about how strong the legs of Behemoth are!
</para>
  <para style="q2">And the muscles in its belly are very powerful.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 40:17"/>Behemoth can make its tail as stiff as the branch of a cedar tree and hold it up in the air.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It has very strong legs.
<verse eid="JOB 40:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 40:18"/>Its thigh bones are so strong that they are like tubes made of bronze.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Its legs are so strong that they are like bars made of iron.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 40:19"/>Behemoth is one of the strongest animals that I made.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I gave it sharp tusks so that it could cut down plants and eat them.
<verse eid="JOB 40:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 40:20"/>It needs those tusks because it comes out of the river where it lives in order to eat the plants that grow nearby.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But other animals do not have to be afraid of it, so those animals do not run away or hide.
<verse eid="JOB 40:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 40:21"/>Behemoth lies under the trees that grow near the river where it lives.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It sometimes shelters in the tall reeds that grow in the swamps of the river.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 40:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 40:22"/>Behemoth stays in the shade of trees that grow near the river.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It stays in the midst of the trees that grow near the water.
<verse eid="JOB 40:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 40:23"/>Even when the river in which it lives become strong and turbulent, this does not disturb Behemoth.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It does not mind even if the level of the water in the river rises very high.
<verse eid="JOB 40:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 40:24"/>No one could capture Behemoth even if he could get close enough to stand right in front of it.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one can make Behemoth a tame animal.

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 40:24"/><verse eid="JOB 40:24"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 40"/>
  <chapter number="41" style="c" sid="JOB 41"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 41:1"/>{And now I want you to think about another great animal that I created, Leviathan.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">You cannot catch Leviathan with a fishhook,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and you cannot tie rope around its jaws to hold them closed!
<verse eid="JOB 41:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 41:2"/>You cannot hook its jaws to catch it
</para>
  <para style="q2">and then put cords through its nose to control it.

<verse eid="JOB 41:2"/></para>
  <para style="b"/>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 41:3"/>Leviathan would certainly not plead with you to act mercifully toward it!
</para>
  <para style="q2">It would not use sweet talk to try to get you not to harm it!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 41:4"/>Leviathan would certainly not make an agreement to work for you
</para>
  <para style="q2">as your slave for as long as it lived!
<verse eid="JOB 41:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 41:5"/>You could not turn it into a pet, as people do with birds!
</para>
  <para style="q2">You could not put a leash around its neck so that the young women in your household could take it for walks!
<verse eid="JOB 41:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 41:6"/>People who work together to catch fish could never catch Leviathan and then decide what share of it belonged to each of them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They would never get the chance to cut Leviathan into pieces and then sell its meat!

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:6"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 41:7"/>People cannot throw harpoons hard enough at Leviathan to pierce its hide!
</para>
  <para style="q2">People cannot throw fishing spears into its head!
<verse eid="JOB 41:7"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 41:8"/>If you try to fight with Leviathan, it will give you a battle that you will never forget!
</para>
  <para style="q2">You will never try to do that again!
<verse eid="JOB 41:8"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 41:9"/>It is useless even to hope to subdue Leviathan.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Just the sight of it is enough to make a person become so afraid that he falls down.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:9"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 41:10"/>No one is so foolhardy that he would wake up Leviathan if it were sleeping. {It would angrily attack him.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">Since that is true, it is even more foolhardy to challenge me{, as you have done}.
<verse eid="JOB 41:10"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 41:11"/>{Even if people are righteous,} they have not done anything for me
</para>
  <para style="q2">that obligates me to make only good things happen to them.
</para>
  <para style="q1">Everything in the created world belongs to me{, so no one can give me anything that I have to repay.}

<verse eid="JOB 41:11"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 41:12"/>And now I want to speak to you further.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{You said that you wanted to wake up Leviathan! As I said, that would be very foolish.}
</para>
  <para style="q2">It has agile legs and great strength and a graceful shape.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:12"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 41:13"/>It has a tough hide that no one can strip off.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It has powerful jaws.
<verse eid="JOB 41:13"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 41:14"/>Those jaws have terrible teeth in them!
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one can pry them open when they are closed{, and no one can release them when they bite onto something.}
<verse eid="JOB 41:14"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 41:15"/>It has rows of scales on its back
</para>
  <para style="q2">with no gaps between them.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:15"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 41:16"/>Those scales are so close together
</para>
  <para style="q2">that it seems as if not even air can get between them.
<verse eid="JOB 41:16"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 41:17"/>Those scales join very closely to each other
</para>
  <para style="q2">and they do not pull apart.
<verse eid="JOB 41:17"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="JOB 41:18"/>When Leviathan makes a loud sound, fire comes out of its mouth as well as noise.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It has bright, scary eyes that seem to glow with fire.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:18"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="JOB 41:19"/>When it breathes,
</para>
  <para style="q2">it shoots out flames and fiery sparks.
<verse eid="JOB 41:19"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="JOB 41:20"/>Smoke pours out of its nostrils
</para>
  <para style="q2">the way steam pours out of a pot of boiling water over a hot fire.
<verse eid="JOB 41:20"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="JOB 41:21"/>Flames shoot out from its mouth,
</para>
  <para style="q2">so when it breathes on wood, the wood catches fire.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:21"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="JOB 41:22"/>Its neck is very strong.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Wherever it goes, it makes people very afraid.
<verse eid="JOB 41:22"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="JOB 41:23"/>Leviathan does not have a soft belly as other animals do.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Scales wrap around its belly and hold it tightly to its body.
<verse eid="JOB 41:23"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="JOB 41:24"/>It does not feel any compassion,
</para>
  <para style="q2">any more than a stone would feel compassion
</para>
  <para style="q2">or the lower millstone on which people grind grain would feel compassion.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:24"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="JOB 41:25"/>When Leviathan rises up out of the ocean,
</para>
  <para style="q2">the bravest people become terrified.
</para>
  <para style="q1">When sailors see it churning up the water,
</para>
  <para style="q2">they sail away as fast as they can.
<verse eid="JOB 41:25"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="JOB 41:26"/>People cannot injure it with swords.
</para>
  <para style="q2">They cannot injure it with spears, arrows, or other weapons that have sharp points, either.
<verse eid="JOB 41:26"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="JOB 41:27"/>An iron weapon could not hurt it any more than straw could.
</para>
  <para style="q2">A bronze weapon could not hurt it any more than a weapon made of rotten wood.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:27"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="JOB 41:28"/>Shooting arrows at it does not cause it to flee.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Hurling stones at it from a sling is like hurling bits of chaff at it.
<verse eid="JOB 41:28"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="JOB 41:29"/>A club that someone might use against it would not hurt it any more than chaff would.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It can ignore any spear that someone might throw at it.
<verse eid="JOB 41:29"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="JOB 41:30"/>Scales as sharp as broken pieces of pottery cover its belly.
</para>
  <para style="q2">When it drags itself through the mud,
</para>
  <para style="q2">these scales tear up the ground.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:30"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="JOB 41:31"/>As it swims through the deep water of the ocean,
</para>
  <para style="q2">it churns it into foam,
</para>
  <para style="q2">as if it were boiling water or ointment that someone was stirring.
<verse eid="JOB 41:31"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="JOB 41:32"/>As it swims through the water, it creates a glistening wake.
</para>
  <para style="q2">The churning water is like white hair on top of the deep sea.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 41:32"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="JOB 41:33"/>There is nothing in the world like Leviathan.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I created it in such a way that it would not be afraid of anything.
<verse eid="JOB 41:33"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="JOB 41:34"/>Nothing else scares it, no matter how powerful that thing may be.
</para>
  <para style="q2">It is greater than the greatest of other creatures.”

<ms style="ts"/>

<verse eid="JOB 41:34"/><verse eid="JOB 41:34"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 41"/>
  <chapter number="42" style="c" sid="JOB 42"/>
  <para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="JOB 42:1"/>Then Job replied to Yahweh. He said,
<verse eid="JOB 42:1"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="JOB 42:2"/>“Now I realize that you can do anything you want.
</para>
  <para style="q2">No one can stop you from doing what you want to do.
<verse eid="JOB 42:2"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="JOB 42:3"/>You told me that I had been speaking ignorantly, and that as a result, I was making it harder for people to understand your actions.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I will admit that I was speaking about things that I did not understand.
</para>
  <para style="q2">Those things were very amazing,
</para>
  <para style="q2">and I did not really know what I was talking about.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 42:3"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="JOB 42:4"/>You told me that I should listen and let you talk because you were going to ask me some questions. You wanted me to try to answer them.
</para>
  <para style="q2">{But I will admit that I am not able to answer the questions you asked.}
<verse eid="JOB 42:4"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="JOB 42:5"/>I had previously heard many things about you.
</para>
  <para style="q2">But now I have actually seen you!
<verse eid="JOB 42:5"/></para>
  <para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="JOB 42:6"/>Therefore I am ashamed of myself for what I said.
</para>
  <para style="q2">I am sitting in dust and ashes to show that I am sorry for what I said.”

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 42:6"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="JOB 42:7"/>When Yahweh had finished speaking with Job, he spoke to Eliphaz, Job’s friend from the town of Teman. He said, “I am very angry with you and your two friends. I am angry because you did not say true things about me the way Job did. He serves me faithfully.
<verse eid="JOB 42:7"/><verse number="8" style="v" sid="JOB 42:8"/>So now you must bring seven young bulls and seven rams to Job, that man who serves me faithfully. While he is with you, kill these animals and burn them as a sacrifice for yourselves. Then Job will pray for you, and I will do what he asks me to do for you. I will not punish you for speaking foolishly about me. You deserve for me to punish you because you did not say the right things about me as Job did. He serves me faithfully.”
<verse eid="JOB 42:8"/><verse number="9" style="v" sid="JOB 42:9"/>So Eliphaz, Job’s friend from the town of Teman; Bildad, Job’s friend from the Shuhite people group; and Zophar, Job’s friend from the town of Naamah, did what Yahweh had commanded them to do. Then Job prayed for his friends, and Yahweh forgave them as Job asked him to do.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 42:9"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="JOB 42:10"/>After Job had prayed for his three friends, Yahweh healed him and made him wealthy again. Yahweh gave him twice as much wealth as he had before.
<verse eid="JOB 42:10"/><verse number="11" style="v" sid="JOB 42:11"/>Then Job’s family and friends came to his house and had a feast with him. All of his brothers and sisters came, and all of his former acquaintances also came. They cheered him up now that all of the troubles were over that Yahweh had allowed to happen to him. Each of them gave Job a valuable piece of silver and a gold earring.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 42:11"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="JOB 42:12"/>Yahweh blessed Job more in the second part of his life than he had blessed him in the first part of his life. Job acquired 14, 000 sheep, 6, 000 camels, 1, 000 pairs of oxen, and 1, 000 female donkeys.
<verse eid="JOB 42:12"/><verse number="13" style="v" sid="JOB 42:13"/>Job also had seven more sons and three more daughters.
<verse eid="JOB 42:13"/><verse number="14" style="v" sid="JOB 42:14"/>He named the first daughter Jemimah. He named the second daughter Keziah. And he named the third daughter Keren Happuch.

<ms style="ts"/>
<verse eid="JOB 42:14"/><verse number="15" style="v" sid="JOB 42:15"/>Job’s three daughters were the most beautiful women in the whole land of Uz. As their father, Job declared that his daughters would each receive an inheritance from him, just as their brothers would.

<verse eid="JOB 42:15"/></para>
  <para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="JOB 42:16"/>After God restored Job’s prosperity, he lived for 140 more years. His new children had children of their own. Then his grandchildren had children of their own, so Job was even able to get to know his great-grandchildren.
<verse eid="JOB 42:16"/><verse number="17" style="v" sid="JOB 42:17"/>Job finally died after living a good long life.
<verse eid="JOB 42:17"/></para>
  <chapter eid="JOB 42"/>
</usx>
